\par\par \b \uc2 Travel Crunch Pinches San Francisco Hotels\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Mehta \par \uc2 733 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 San Francisco\uc2 's hospitality business imploded during the pandemic. Now, its hotel owners are drowning in bad debt as never before.\par \par \uc2 In the city's metropolitan area, the delinquency rate among commercial mortgage-backed security loans for the lodging sector skyrocketed to 41.6% in June from 5.7% in June 2023, according to data from real-estate analytics firm Trepp. It is the largest increase across the country's 25 largest metro areas.\par \par \uc2 The sharp drop-off in visitors since before the pandemic is squeezing the city's hospitality sector. Weekend hotel occupancy in June, a rough proxy for leisure travel, is down around 22% since 2019 in the San Francisco-San Mateo region, versus 4% nationwide, according to data firm \uc2 CoStar Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For hospitality, 2024 "is definitely a difficult year," said Anna Marie Presutti, interim president of the San Francisco Travel Association.\par \par \uc2 Many other indicators are worsening or failing to improve. Leisure travel hasn't picked up from last year's lows, despite recovery in other markets, with tourists put off by quality-of-life issues. And while the city expects conferences to turn around next year, these events are down from last year and prepandemic.\par \par \uc2 Fewer conferences and visitors mean fewer room nights. The city's two largest hotels, the Hilton Parc 55 and Hilton San Francisco Union Square, combined have lost $1 billion in value, according to the \uc2 Kroll Bond Rating Agency\uc2 , which valued them at $553.8 million.\par \par \uc2 Park Hotels & Resorts\uc2 , the real-estate investment trust that owned the two hotels, appears relieved to drop the two properties from its portfolio.\par \par \uc2 Exiting the two hotels "meaningfully improved our balance sheet and operating metrics," said \uc2 Tom Baltimore\uc2 , the firm's chief executive during a first-quarter earnings call. The San Francisco market, alongside Los Angeles, will "probably lag for some time," he said.\par \par \uc2 Hotel staffers, bearing the brunt of the downturn, say they are facing a reduction in hours and taking on second jobs.\par \par \uc2 This month, contracts covering the Bay Area's roughly 10,000 hotel workers expire, and 3,000 of those workers overwhelmingly voted to authorize a potential strike, their union announced.\par \par \uc2 Presutti took the helm of the San Francisco Travel Association after her predecessor resigned in May. Weeks before his resignation, he offered a pessimistic projection: The city's conference business would take until 2028 or 2029 to recover. Now, Presutti says that is a realistic estimate for the city's laggard tourism recovery.\par \par \uc2 Sluggish leisure travel has been a persistent concern. More Americans are capitalizing on the strong dollar by traveling abroad, and a return to the office means more travel for work. Both of these may substitute for domestic leisure travel, said Emmy Hise, senior director of hospitality analytics at \uc2 CoStar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, tourists from China, a major source of visitors, are deterred by the strong dollar and economic uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Still, European travelers, particularly visitors from the U.K., Germany and France, are starting to return to San Francisco. Visitors from India also are increasing, according to the city's travel association.\par \par \uc2 Alex Bastian, chief executive of the city's hotel council, said the industry is going through "unparalleled challenges." But he is optimistic about an increasingly cleaner, tourist- and business-friendly city attracting more visitors.\par \par \uc2 "Things are moving in the right direction," Bastian said.\par \par \uc2 First-time visitors to the Bay Area for conferences tend to rate their experiences as positive. Those visitors often end up returning as vacationers or adding extra hotel days to their travels. But as conferences from companies like \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  and \uc2 Red Hat\uc2  depart the Moscone Center, San Francisco's main convention center, the city loses out on additional revenue from extended tourism.\par \par \uc2 Lukas Karlsson, a cloud consultant who has attended the \uc2 Google\uc2  Cloud Next Conference in San Francisco regularly since 2016, typically adds on nights to his Bay Area hotel to spend time with family.\par \par \uc2 When the conference moved to Las Vegas this year, Karlsson was rather disappointed.\par \par \uc2 He would be in Vegas the following week for four days of shows by rock band Phish at the city's Sphere. Tired from the city, he didn't extend his stay through intermediate days -- he instead left for Seattle and came back for the conference. "I could easily do two weeks in San Francisco," he said. Not in Las Vegas.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
