\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing Factories Remain Source Of Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Tangel \par \uc2 918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Factories are still a major source of safety concerns at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , according to testimony from current and former company employees, contractors and regulators at a federal hearing this week.\par \par \uc2 In testimony over two days, one worker said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  managers displayed a "push-push-push" attitude to complete work in factories. The employee also said speaking out about safety problems risks punitive reassignments. A former \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  official also said \uc2 Boeing\uc2  makes improvements in factories only after regulators get tough.\par \par \uc2 The hearing was part of the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 's probe into why a door plug flew off a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 MAX plane midair on Jan. 5. Accident investigators have said the jet left the company's Renton, Wash., factory without critical bolts to hold the fuselage panel in place. \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight 1282 landed in Portland, Ore., roughly 30 minutes after takeoff without any major injuries to passengers and crew.\par \par \uc2 The door-plug debacle spawned more stringent federal oversight of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's manufacturing operations and leadership changes at the plane maker. The company recently pleaded guilty to conspiring to mislead air-safety regulators in connection with deadly MAX crashes in 2018 and 2019.\par \par \uc2 "A healthy management-employee relationship is based on trust, treatment and transparency," \uc2 NTSB\uc2  Chair Jennifer Homendy said Wednesday. "I don't see these here."\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executives testifying this week said the company needs to accelerate changes to improve its safety culture.\par \par \uc2 Problems associated with inexperienced employees, out-of-sequence production work and hard-driving managers weigh on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's ability to operate its factories properly, according to testimony gathered in the probe, even while regulators and company executives press for improvements.\par \par \uc2 A Seattle-area employee on a crew that specializes in removing and reinstalling door plugs described \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's safety culture as "garbage. Nobody's accountable." He cited persistent debris and tools strewn about the factory.\par \par \uc2 Workers are pushed to keep production at a steady pace, the employee said. "Here, it's just push-push-push, push-push-push, push-push-push, you know," the employee said.\par \par \uc2 The employee said he didn't think \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's internal reporting system protected employees from retaliation and complained that he had been reassigned during the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigation to an area he described as "literally in a jail, in a cage."\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Lund, a senior vice president for quality at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , said she wasn't aware the area was known as "\uc2 Boeing\uc2  prison." She said the company typically reassigns employees during safety investigations and said the employee in question was given a lateral transfer with the same pay, shift and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Lloyd Catlin, an official in \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's machinist union, said Wednesday that such transfers send a clear punitive message to the workforce: "We see it all the time."\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  conducted a safety risk assessment on how workers remove parts like door plugs during the manufacturing process without proper documentation. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  had previously prodded the company to address the problem.\par \par \uc2 The plane maker had undertaken corrective actions and issued a quality alert on the subject in July 2023, just two months before the jet involved in the accident rolled out of its Renton factory.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers cited confusion about requirements for conducting "removals" of critical airplane parts. The crew lead said the manager of the group involved with removing and reinstalling door plugs was new to his role and "doesn't really understand doors."\par \par \uc2 Lund, the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  executive, acknowledged the company needs to do better with removals. Aerospace manufacturing requires extensive documentation, particularly when critical parts are removed from aircraft during production.\par \par \uc2 "We need to do more" to simplify the process and ensure that employees understand it, Lund said.\par \par \uc2 Jim Phoenix, who managed the \uc2 FAA\uc2  office overseeing \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's manufacturing until he retired in December, said it is a heavy lift to prod the company to improve. "You need a lot of leverage to get \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to change and then when \uc2 Boeing\uc2  changes, it's very slow and it took a long time for them to really understand that their quality system needed to improve."\par \par \uc2 Phoenix said he saw improvements after the agency forced \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to add back quality inspections. "There was a lot of improvement that was needed," he told \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigators. "I'm sure there still is."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  accident exposed other manufacturing flaws. Nearly all the oxygen generators above where passengers sit shifted during flight, because of defective adhesive, according to the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 . After the accident, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  ordered airlines to inspect oxygen canisters on more than 2,600 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737s, noting the potential risk to passengers who could need assistance breathing during an in-flight emergency.\par \par \uc2 Paul Wright, a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  safety executive, said the company's analysis found that the shifting of the canisters didn't prevent passengers from getting oxygen.\par \par \uc2 Separately, a warning light about pressurization that activated before the door plug blew off was the result of a faulty computer chip, according to the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 . Earlier in the investigation, there were questions about whether the alert was an early sign that the fuselage panel wasn't being properly installed.\par \par \uc2 It turns out to have been luck that no one was seated next to the door plug. Two seats next to it blew out midflight.\par \par \uc2 The passengers who were assigned to seats 26A and 26B "were late arriving to the airport and were rebooked on a later flight," according to the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Sharon Terlep and Alison Sider contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240808ek880000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
