\par\par \b \uc2 SunPower\uc2  Files for Chapter 11 Bankruptcy --- Deal is set to sell some of it assets to its competitor, \uc2 Complete Solaria\b0\par\par\uc2 By Colin Kellaher \par \uc2 255 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Solar-energy company \uc2 SunPower\uc2  has filed for chapter 11 bankruptcy with a deal in hand to sell some of its assets to competitor \uc2 Complete Solaria\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SunPower\uc2  late Monday said it agreed to sell the assets associated with its Blue Raven Solar business, as well as its new-homes business and its noninstalling dealer network, for $45 million to \uc2 Complete Solaria\uc2 , which will serve as the stalking horse, or lead, bidder in a court-supervised auction for the assets. \uc2 SunPower\uc2  added it plans to continue a sale process for its remaining assets and to wind down its operations.\par \par \uc2 SunPower\uc2  in April unveiled plans to cut nearly a quarter of its workforce as part of a restructuring aimed at reducing operating costs and improving the economics of its business as the Richmond, Calif., company shifted to a low fixed-cost model.\par \par \uc2 SunPower\uc2  at the time said the solar market's recovery has been slower than expected, and that the company was simplifying its business structure and moving away from areas where it has been unable to sustain profitable operations.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 SunPower\uc2  in July told solar dealers and installers that it would no longer lease panels to homeowners, a move that \uc2 Guggenheim Securities\uc2  analyst \uc2 Joe Osha\uc2  said made it likely that the company would have to stop operating entirely.\par \par \uc2 SunPower\uc2  shares closed Tuesday down 43% at 46.4 cents each. Shareholders are generally wiped out in bankruptcy cases.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240807ek8700036\par }\p