\par\par \b \uc2 Musk's X Accuses Ad Group Of Boycott\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alyssa Lukpat, Joseph De Avila and Dana Mattioli \par \uc2 582 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's social-media company X filed a federal antitrust lawsuit against an advertising industry coalition, saying companies in the group illegally boycotted the platform.\par \par \uc2 X alleged that companies including \uc2 CVS Health\uc2 , Mars and \uc2 Unilever\uc2  conspired to withhold billions in advertising dollars to force the platform formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to maintain certain safety standards, according to the complaint filed Tuesday in Texas' northern district.\par \par \uc2 The boycott began in November 2022 after Musk acquired \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the lawsuit said. The coalition started the boycott due to a concern that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  would change its content and safety standards under Musk's leadership, according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 At least 18 brands associated with the coalition stopped advertising on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  from November through December 2022, according to the lawsuit. Dozens more of the coalition's members cut back spending on ads through 2023.\par \par \uc2 X said the coalition violated antitrust laws by having its members agree to a boycott, which X called a "coercive exercise of market power."\par \par \uc2 The companies cited in the X lawsuit were part of the \uc2 World Federation of Advertisers\uc2 , which has dozens of members. The group has an initiative called the Global Alliance for Responsible Media, which pushes for safeguards to remove harmful content, such as child sexual abuse material and content promoting terrorism, so it isn't placed next to ads from their members.\par \par \uc2 The Global Alliance for Responsible Media noted "the massive economic harm imposed on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  by the boycott," the lawsuit said. "The boycott and its effects continue to this day, despite X applying brand safety standards comparable to those of its competitors."\par \par \uc2 After acquiring the platform in 2022, Musk pledged to loosen content restrictions to encourage free speech. Outside researchers and critics have said Musk's platform has allowed the proliferation of hateful and false content. The company has disputed such criticisms and has said it has made progress on reducing the spread of hateful content.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Federation of Advertisers\uc2  and the other defendants didn't respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit doesn't resemble a typical antitrust claim, said \uc2 Stephen Calkins\uc2 , a law professor at \uc2 Wayne State University\uc2  who specializes in antitrust law.\par \par \uc2 "If people have simply switched purchasing ads from one platform to another, it's not clear how that is going to be harming competition and ending up at higher prices," he said.\par \par \uc2 In the complaint, X argues social-media platforms that comply with GARM's standards are able to avoid competing on prices with X because those platforms "know that GARM-member advertisers and advertising agencies have agreed not to purchase advertising from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (and now X)."\par \par \uc2 Over the last year in meetings on Capitol Hill with Rep. Jim Jordan (R., Ohio), and others, Musk complained about advertisers boycotting X and complained about Global Alliance for Responsible Media. In May, Jordan subpoenaed GARM for documents for evidence of anticonservative bias.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit references that report, which found that GARM may have violated antitrust laws by withholding advertising spending from social-media platforms and conservative media outlets.\par \par \uc2 Advertisers have fled X since Musk bought the platform in 2022, partially in response to Musk's inflammatory posts. Last year, several large advertisers ditched the platform after the billionaire described an antisemitic post as "the actual truth." At a conference, he said advertisers pulling their ads from X can "go f--- yourself."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Alexa Corse contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240807ek870003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
