\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: A Death From Heat Offers a Warning\b0\par\par\uc2 By Arian Campo-Flores \par \uc2 939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justin "Cory" Foster, a lineman who often traveled to storm-ravaged communities to help restore electricity, was used to working in searing weather atop utility poles.\par \par \uc2 But as the heat index climbed to 113 degrees Fahrenheit on a job in Marshall, Texas, last year, his head hurt, his legs cramped and he threw up, Foster told his fiancee, Amanda Hightower, on a FaceTime call that evening. Co-workers drove him back to their hotel, where he drank fluids and took a cold shower.\par \par \uc2 "I got too hot today," he told her. Hightower, 28 years old, said she wasn't too worried because she was accustomed to seeing him come home flushed and sweaty. She didn't imagine the severity of the ordeal he was suffering.\par \par \uc2 It proved to be deadly.\par \par \uc2 Workers in sweltering settings, from farms and construction sites to foundries and warehouses, have long been vulnerable. Now, as climate change drives temperatures to new highs, they are more susceptible than ever.\par \par \uc2 What happened to Foster, a healthy 35-year-old, underscores the perils. Work-related deaths from environmental heat are trending up in the U.S. -- to 43 in 2022, the most recent data, from 31 in 2012, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Between 2011 and 2020, work-related heat injuries and illnesses averaged 3,389 a year, \uc2 BLS\uc2  data show -- likely a vast underestimate, an \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  panel said.\par \par \uc2 Heat waves across the U.S. have become longer, more frequent and more intense over the past six decades, according to the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2 . The global surface temperature in June was the warmest on record for that month. It was the 13th consecutive record month, \uc2 NOAA\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Last month the Biden administration proposed the first federal safety standard to protect workers from extreme heat. California recently approved rules to protect indoor workers from extreme heat -- though Florida this year barred local governments from requiring heat protections.\par \par \uc2 In Arcadia, Fla., a 42-year-old farmworker died from heat stroke in December after picking oranges under the sun. In Pontiac, Ill., a 36-year-old roofer died from heat-related illness in August 2023.\par \par \uc2 Foster, a lineman with \uc2 Appalachian Power\uc2  in West Virginia, got a call to deploy to Marshall after storms toppled power poles. Arriving with fellow crew members on June 18 of last year, he told Hightower that they would start the next morning installing new poles and lines in a swampy area.\par \par \uc2 "Please be careful," Hightower said she told him.\par \par \uc2 Linemen's duties are physically demanding, and performed in thick rubber gloves and sleeves to guard against contact with energized lines and flame-retardant clothing that isn't very breathable.\par \par \uc2 "It ain't nothing but a great big sweatsuit," said Bill Bosch, founder of the National Association of Journeymen Linemen.\par \par \uc2 Foster previously experienced episodes of heat exhaustion, co-workers later told an \uc2 OSHA\uc2  compliance officer, according to an agency inspection report. That day in Marshall, he complained of chest pains and other signs of severe heat stress, but declined to be taken to a hospital, they said.\par \par \uc2 When the body overheats, it begins to lose control of its cooling mechanisms, said Brenda Jacklitsch, a health scientist at the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2 . A person's condition can deteriorate quickly or slowly, from weakness and dizziness to cognitive changes such as confusion and slurred speech, she said. Eventually, organs can start to shut down.\par \par \uc2 Crew members drove Foster to the hotel at around 6:30 p.m. and helped him hydrate, shed his clothes and cool off in one of their rooms, Hightower said he told her. He declined to join them for dinner.\par \par \uc2 Foster headed up a stairwell to his room, where he said the air conditioning wasn't working, according to Hightower. He put on some basketball shorts and made a FaceTime call to Hightower a little before 7:30 p.m., she said.\par \par \uc2 Foster looked sweaty and sticky and complained that his legs were cramping, Hightower said. She urged him to drink water, which he did, but he said his stomach hurt each time he took a sip. He told her he needed another cold shower. She asked him to text or call her as soon as he was done.\par \par \uc2 Around 7:45 p.m., Hightower sent him a text: "I love you babe I wish I could take your cramps away." Foster didn't respond.\par \par \uc2 When Foster's co-workers returned from dinner at about 8:25 p.m., his roommate found him unresponsive on the bathroom floor, said Judge John Oswalt, a justice of the peace in Harrison County, Texas, who investigated the incident. Emergency medical services responders said Foster wasn't breathing and had no pulse, according to Oswalt. A little after 9 p.m., they stopped trying to resuscitate him.\par \par \uc2 The cause of death was complications of overheating, according to an autopsy report.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OSHA\uc2  compliance officer inspected the work site and reviewed \uc2 Appalachian Power\uc2 's policies, according to the inspection report. \uc2 OSHA\uc2  closed the case without issuing a citation. \uc2 Appalachian Power\uc2  declined to comment but last year said it was investigating.\par \par \uc2 Foster is survived by an 11-year-old son and a 3-year-old daughter. His family wrote in an obituary that he loved chasing storms around the country to help restore power.\par \par \uc2 When Hightower received a bag of Foster's belongings shipped to her after his death, she said she took out his clothing. "His pants were still wet with sweat," she said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich563\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal8445
