\par\par \b \uc2 FedEx\uc2  Cuts Back on Duplicate Trucks in Logistics Challenge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Esther Fung \par \uc2 813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Federal Express, the network that integrates FedEx's ground and express networks, employs around 430,000 people around the world. A Business & Finance article on Monday about FedEx incorrectly said Federal Express employs around 430,000 people in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ Aug. 7, 2024)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  for years operated almost indistinguishable Ground and Express networks, their trucks sometimes delivering packages to the same doorstep within minutes of each other. Merging the two has revealed just how different the halves really were.\par \par \uc2 The parcel giant has embarked on a yearslong overhaul that will combine two networks with more than 500,000 workers that have operated side by side for decades. The overlapping structure racked up costs that were hard to justify as more of the business shifted to delivering orders from \uc2 eBay\uc2  or a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  store instead of time-sensitive packages to a hospital or office tower.\par \par \uc2 It is the equivalent of a corporate merger -- unifying two streams of packages, workers and facilities -- while keeping the trucks arriving on time. Last fiscal year, Ground hauled in $34 billion in revenue and generated five times as much operating profit as Express, which recorded $41 billion in revenue. Express uses \uc2 FedEx\uc2  jets, trucks and employees to deliver its boxes. Ground outsources its deliveries to contractors that operate local routes.\par \par \uc2 "Bringing them into a single transportation system is a daunting task," said \uc2 John Smith\uc2 , a \uc2 FedEx\uc2  executive tasked with working out the kinks. (He is unrelated to \uc2 Fred Smith\uc2 , the founder and executive chairman of \uc2 FedEx\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 The Memphis, Tenn., company has started closing some facilities and shifting work to nearby locations. It is parking planes and cutting jobs. Since it started integrating the two units in 2022, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  has discovered some wrinkles, such as tech snafus and productivity issues. In a few locations, it had to retain drivers to handle shipments with tight deadlines.\par \par \uc2 "One of the things that we learned from this is to have what we're now calling 'designated couriers,' " said \uc2 John Smith\uc2 . \uc2 FedEx\uc2  needs the drivers picking up packages from airplanes to make tight delivery deadlines, he added.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and rival \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  have been under pressure as package volumes declined following the pandemic surge in e-commerce.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2  shares recently plunged after \uc2 UPS\uc2  warned of weaker profit margins. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  also has to contend with a leadership transition from its founder and the arrival of activist investor D.E. Shaw, which took a stake in 2022 and has appointed three members to the \uc2 FedEx\uc2  board.\par \par \uc2 In June, \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said it was assessing options for its third business unit, Freight, which investors expect to be spun off as a publicly traded company. Freight is the largest operator in the less-than-truckload sector, with about $9 billion in annual revenue. The less-than-truckload business combines shipments from multiple customers on a single truck.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  executives had long defended the duplication, saying the networks served different customers, with Express deliveries commanding higher profits because of its time-sensitive service. This irked investors who thought \uc2 UPS\uc2  had a more cost-efficient, one-truck-one-neighborhood delivery model.\par \par \uc2 In June 2023, under new Chief Executive \uc2 Raj Subramaniam\uc2 , \uc2 FedEx\uc2  said it would officially change course. The growth of e-commerce and the volume of home deliveries pushed the company to increase utilization of trucks and get rid of surplus equipment and facilities. This would improve density, efficiency and the customer experience, Subramaniam said.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is introducing the changes in smaller distribution centers such as Bozeman, Mont., and Starkville, Miss., that have fewer routes and lower daily parcel volumes. Executives say they are learning from the early moves and adjusting plans for the integration in bigger cities.\par \par \uc2 Initially, for example, some facility managers experimented with having Express packages delivered before Ground packages. But they learned it was more efficient to consolidate packages into smaller delivery areas instead.\par \par \uc2 In some markets, including Hawaii, Alaska and Canada, Express operations took over Ground deliveries because shipments in these regions rely more on planes. In the Lower 48 U.S. states, Ground contractors are more frequently taking over Express volume.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  has told investors that the integration, together with other consolidation efforts, will cost around $1.5 billion in the current fiscal year that ends May 2025.\par \par \uc2 "You're taking freight from one facility to another. You're taking people and moving them into different operating cultures with different management and processes," said J. Bruce Chan, an analyst at \uc2 Stifel\uc2  who covers \uc2 UPS\uc2 , \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and other freight companies. "There is a lot of room for operational missteps."\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  has closed dozens of buildings, retired 32 jets and reduced its U.S. head count by around 22,000 through attrition and layoffs. The new network, called \uc2 Federal Express\uc2 , now employs around 430,000 people in the U.S.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich425\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6375
