\par\par \b \uc2 Most Popular Trades Of 2024 Slump --- Some investors worry \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has waited too long to cut rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Pitcher \par \uc2 837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Fears of slowing growth are rattling financial markets around the world, spurring investors to unwind many of 2024's most popular trades.\par \par \uc2 A volatile week in markets turned uglier on Friday after a weaker-than-expected July payrolls report showed job growth slowing and unemployment rising to the highest level since 2021.\par \par \uc2 Stocks and bond yields dove, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average losing more than 600 points. Oil prices slid, as traders bet slowing growth would erode demand for crude.\par \par \uc2 The stock declines extended a fall that began a day earlier, after data showed weakness in manufacturing and construction. But the reversal of some of the year's most-popular trades was already under way, with investors shifting positions ahead of potential interest-rate cuts from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Now, just days after the Fed held short-term rates steady at its July policy meeting, many on Wall Street are worried that the central bank has waited too long to begin cutting rates and that the economy might suffer. By Friday, traders were pricing in overwhelming odds that the Fed will cut rates by half a percentage point in September. Few had made that wager on Wednesday after Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said that officials needed to see more data.\par \par \uc2 Friday's jobs report was "the first clear sign of employment slowing across virtually every metric," said \uc2 Rick Rieder\uc2 , \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 's chief investment officer of global fixed income. "The Fed-funds rate is clearly too restrictive relative to inflation that is trending in the low-2s with slack building in the labor force."\par \par \uc2 The market swings are the latest in a bruising series for what were recently some of Wall Street's favorite bets.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year Treasury, which falls when bond prices rise, just posted its largest one-week decline -- retreating below 4%, to 3.795%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  -- since the pandemic market panic of 2020. Japan's Nikkei 225 slumped 5.8% Friday and is down almost 15% from a record in June, while the yen has surged against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 And the Magnificent Seven tech titans that have powered most of this year's stock gains have had a rough few weeks, shedding more than $1 trillion of market value combined.\par \par \uc2 Second-quarter earnings reports have so far disappointed investors, who had high expectations, particularly for companies that are investing a lot in artificial intelligence. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , two heavy AI spenders, both sold off after reporting results.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, chief executives are sounding the alarm that the economy could be worse than backward-looking data indicate.\par \par \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's CEO \uc2 Chris Kempczinski\uc2  said recently that a slowdown in visits from lower-income consumers has deepened this year.\par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  shares dropped 8.8% Friday after the company projected weaker-than-expected revenue growth.\par \par \uc2 And a particularly bad earnings report -- including struggles in the AI and data-center business -- sent Intel shares plunging 26%, their worst day since at least 1985.\par \par \uc2 "There's now a drumbeat of skepticism about the benefits of AI and whether it will actually deliver profits and productivity at the level that's been promoted," said Yung-Yu Ma, chief investment officer for BMO Wealth Management in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "It's not surprising that the ebb of enthusiasm is going to take place at the same time we've seen some economic weakness and concern that the Fed is behind the curve," he said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also said that hedge funds that went all-in on some of this year's most popular trades -- such as buying big tech stocks or betting on declines in the Japanese yen -- are now exacerbating market moves.\par \par \uc2 When a hedge fund has a highly concentrated position that starts to fall, it often needs to start selling assets and cutting risk elsewhere in the portfolio to satisfy its risk models. When a lot of funds have been buying the same things, that process can pressure a variety of investments, including some seemingly remote from the original bet.\par \par \uc2 Macro investors reversing a popular trade in which they borrow yen and swap them for dollars to take advantage of the discrepancy between the two countries' interest rates might have exacerbated a recent climb in the yen, some analysts said. Japan's central bank raised interest rates last week, further contributing to the currency's climb.\par \par \uc2 "The pricing and the acuteness of the market action I think is really indicative of the fact that hedge funds have been caught offside," said Bob Elliott, CEO of asset manager Unlimited.\par \par \uc2 The surge in volatility follows a prolonged period of market calm.\par \par \uc2 The tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index entered correction territory on Friday, or a 10% drop from its recent high, but remains up 12% for the year. The S&P 500 is also up 12% in 2024. A pullback from highs driven by exuberant investors can be healthy.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich472\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal7080
