\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- \uc2 Chevron\uc2  Plans to Relocate to Texas As Regulations Mount in California\b0\par\par\uc2 By Collin Eaton \par \uc2 626 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  is relocating to Texas, deserting California, its home state for more than 140 years, where the business climate has soured for oil companies.\par \par \uc2 The second-largest U.S. oil company said Friday it plans to move its global headquarters to Houston, the U.S. energy industry capital. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  has built a stronghold of about 7,000 employees there, partly from a matriculation of executives and white-collar workers decamping from California.\par \par \uc2 The relocation plans come weeks after billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said X and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  would move their headquarters to Austin, out of California. Musk had moved \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's headquarters to Texas a few years ago. Several other large U.S. companies, including \uc2 Hewlett Packard Enterprise\uc2  and \uc2 Oracle\uc2 , have moved from blue states such as California to red states such as Texas.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mike Wirth\uc2  said the company wants to move its employees to one central hub. He also acknowledged the company has been vocal about its differences of opinion with California on energy policy. "We believe California has a number of policies that raise costs, that hurt consumers, that discourage investment and ultimately we think that's not good for the economy in California and for consumers," Wirth said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Last year, California's attorney general, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , filed a lawsuit against \uc2 Chevron\uc2  and other large oil companies such as \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  and \uc2 Shell\uc2 , making a case that the companies didn't inform the public of the effects of burning fossil fuels on the climate. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  is named in a large number of climate lawsuits across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that climate is a matter that's a global issue and is best addressed through national and global policy engagement, and not through the courts," Wirth said.\par \par \uc2 Some of \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's leadership has long wanted to move the company's headquarters to Texas, but it has held off largely because of its long history in California and its assets there, such as its Richmond, Calif., refinery, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  said in January it would write down as much as $4 billion in assets, mostly in California, citing regulatory challenges there. At the time, it warned against California's so-called margin penalty, which seeks to limit profits from the state's refiners to prevent alleged price gouging. Democratic Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  signed it into law last year.\par \par \uc2 California also has a state corporate income-tax rate of 8.8%. Texas has no such tax.\par \par \uc2 The oil company planted its main offices in the greater Bay Area in 1879, when it was known as Pacific Coast Oil Co. It moved hundreds of employees to its current headquarters, in San Ramon, in 1999, not far from California's tech hubs. But following a reorganization in 2019, its footprint in California began to decline.\par \par \uc2 Wirth and the company's vice chairman, \uc2 Mark Nelson\uc2 , plan to relocate to Houston before the end of the year, the company said, along with a portion of its roughly 2,000 employees in San Ramon, over time. Moving to Houston will put \uc2 Chevron\uc2  closer to key stakeholders, such as partners, vendors, suppliers, and energy companies from which it recruits new blood, Wirth said.\par \par \uc2 The company, which has two refineries and some oil fields in California, expects the shift in its corporate functions to Houston to take five years. It will keep workers who support local operations in San Ramon, where it has moved to smaller offices in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2 's chief rival, \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2 , moved its headquarters from Irving, Texas, to Spring, an area in Harris County north of Houston, last year, consolidating office space at its sprawling campus.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240803ek8300015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
