\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- The Activist Pushing Companies to Ditch DEI --- He has targeted \uc2 Tractor Supply\uc2 , \uc2 Deere\uc2  and more. His list is getting longer.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Pisani and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,287 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It took three weeks of tweets from conservative activist Robby Starbuck for \uc2 Tractor Supply\uc2  to scrap its diversity and inclusion program. Tractor-maker \uc2 Deere\uc2  folded even faster.\par \par \uc2 "Our next company we go after will be shorter than that," Starbuck said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 From his home office outside of Nashville, Tenn., decorated with a massive American flag, Starbuck, who is known for making music videos and the footage that airs in movie theaters before the show that tells viewers to keep quiet and turn off their cellphones, recently decided to build a social-media operation to voice his objections to corporate culture.\par \par \uc2 Starbuck, 35, has launched campaigns to stoke outrage about what he calls companies' "woke" diversity, equity and inclusion initiatives. Many of his 500,000 followers on X have joined in, including retweets from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , building a chorus of criticism that, in part, caused \uc2 Tractor Supply\uc2  and \uc2 Deere\uc2  to abandon some of their efforts aimed at supporting workers from underrepresented backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 Tractor Supply\uc2 , a rural retailer that sells animal feed and workwear, said on X after weeks of pressure on social-media from Starbuck and others: "We have heard from customers that we have disappointed them. We have taken this feedback to heart."\par \par \uc2 Deere\uc2  said last month that it has never had diversity quotas, that it would make sure there are no "socially motivated messages" in its training materials unless required, and that it wouldn't participate in or support "social or cultural awareness parades, festivals, or events."\par \par \uc2 Starbuck doesn't plan to stop anytime soon. His moves capitalize on a growing anxiety inside corporations about coming under attack for taking public stances on hot-button issues, especially as DEI initiatives have started to fall out of favor. Giving in to Starbuck could alienate employees and customers. Not responding might intensify the conflict and lead to a boycott.\par \par \uc2 He says the workplace should be politically neutral, and has at least four other companies that he plans to go after, he said, but declined to name them. His latest target is motorcycle maker \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Everybody should just go to work, do their job, go home," he said. "You want to be an activist in your personal time? That's your business."\par \par \uc2 A Cuban-American, Starbuck says he does it because he's a dad, and he doesn't want his three young children to think they got a job because they are Latino when they get old enough to work. He has a fourth child on the way.\par \par \uc2 Starbuck grew up in Temecula, Calif., with three siblings and his mom, who immigrated to the U.S. from Cuba. He said he took college classes at a local community college in his senior year of high school, and then started recording musician's live performances. Musicians would post the videos on their webpages, and others would ask him to do the same, leading him to eventually start a production company in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 He says he always voted Republican, but a shift happened about seven years ago when he became more socially conservative. "I started to have a yearning for God in my life," he said.\par \par \uc2 He became uncomfortable with sexual innuendo and jokes sprinkled in TV shows, movies and commercials his children were watching. Six years ago, Starbuck, his wife, Landon, and three children moved out of Calabasas, Calif., and headed for Tennessee, where they live outside Nashville in a house with a farm. Part of the home's decor: a giant black-and-white photo of a lion's face that Starbuck said was a gift. "They said it reminded them of my spirit."\par \par \uc2 "We had fully understood we did not fit in that world," he said about California and Hollywood. "Our values and our ideology were so far apart from that industry that it was like we just have to trust God and start over."\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, he has advocated for upholding a state ban on gender-affirming care for transgender children, as well as a law that would allow the death penalty for people convicted of child rape.\par \par \uc2 Musk, the billionaire who owns X and has more than 190 million followers, has replied to Starbuck's posts more than 45 times this year.\par \par \uc2 "Doesn't sound super compatible with their customers," Musk said in response to a tweet by Starbuck about Harley-Davidson's DEI policy.\par \par \uc2 "He's just my friend on X," Starbuck said of Musk. "I'm grateful he reads the stuff that I'm putting out there."\par \par \uc2 Starbuck unsuccessfully tried to run for Congress in Tennessee in 2022. He doesn't plan to run for office again in the near future.\par \par \uc2 "I'm accomplishing so much outside," he said, "why would I tie myself up?"\par \par \uc2 In February, Starbuck released the documentary, "The War On Children," which he sells for up to $15 on his site. He plans to produce more documentaries, he said.\par \par \uc2 It features Starbuck and his wife interviewing people on the same topics he regularly rails against online. Footage of news headlines, including some from far-right outlets like the Gateway Pundit, are spliced in between. Musk, in a post on X, said the documentary is worth watching.\par \par \uc2 "Why is it that our kids today are faced with Pride flags everywhere they go, no matter what, at every company?" Starbuck asks one interview subject in the film.\par \par \uc2 In another segment, Starbuck cites the example of boycotts against Bud Light and Target as models for would-be consumer activists. "Use your money as a weapon," Landon Starbuck said.\par \par \uc2 After Bud Light sent a personalized can to a transgender influencer last year, consumers boycotted the brand, leading to a monthslong sales decline. Once the top-selling beer in America, Bud Light has now fallen to the No. 3 spot behind Modelo Especial and Michelob Ultra, recent sales data show.\par \par \uc2 Target's sales took a hit last summer after it faced criticism over how it handled Gay Pride month displays.\par \par \uc2 Starbuck pays his two full-time employees, who edit videos and conduct research on the companies he tweets about, using money he makes from the $5 a month he charges subscribers on X for bonus videos and other content. He declined to say how much annual revenue he makes from his documentary and X subscriptions.\par \par \uc2 Veteran corporate executives say companies need to be prepared for this kind of scrutiny. Making everyone happy may not be an option.\par \par \uc2 Kelley Johnson, a former chief diversity officer at \uc2 JCPenney\uc2 , said companies should be proactive about adding a DEI component to their businesses -- in case they are targeted by activists -- and should be conscious of the risks associated with cutting such programs. "Ultimately, the question is this: Who do we lose in the process if we decide to end our DEI efforts? What market segment, what employees might we lose?" said Johnson, who now runs Keirus, a consulting firm focused on cultural transformation.\par \par \uc2 In his campaign against \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2 , which he launched in a July 23 X post, Starbuck said the company has supported LGBTQ+ causes and shown "a total commitment to DEI policies."\par \par \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Starbuck said he's "nowhere close to running out of companies, but I really hope I do, because that means that we've forced sanity in the workplace."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Robby Starbuck\par \par \uc2 -- Grew up: In Temecula, Calif.\par \par \uc2 -- His farm: Has two cows and 20 chickens\par \par \uc2 -- Favorite book: The bible\par \par \uc2 -- Children: Three, and a fourth due in October\par \par \uc2 -- Political quest: Unsuccessful bid for Tennessee seat in Congress in 2022\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240803ek830003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
