\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- If These Resumes Could Talk: A Fake Particle Physicist, A Car Wreck and a Forgetful Recruiter --- First impressions can make all the difference in the world. Not always in a good way.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francesca Fontana \par \uc2 847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [If These Resumes Could Talk is a Wall Street Journal feature in which recruiters and headhunters share their wildest and most interesting stories.]\par \par \uc2 Q: What are unusual or most memorable first impressions that candidates or clients made?\par \par \uc2 Black hole in a resume\par \par \uc2 We're focused on tech -- data science -- and we find that candidates are sometimes trying to keyword-load their resumes so that they get past the bots that recruiters use to screen them. Or the resumes are way too long, they put way too many bullet points. And people want to make their resumes stand out, so sometimes people go to the web and just copy stuff.\par \par \uc2 One of the most comical examples we've seen was a candidate who had worked at one of the major U.S. insurance companies. This person's resume had a lot of bullet points, and the very last bullet point was something like, "Analyzed results from the Large Hadron Collider."\par \par \uc2 The Large Hadron Collider was probably in the news around that time -- it was this huge particle collider and there was talk that it was going to create a black hole and destroy the Earth.\par \par \uc2 There's just no way that someone working in insurance would do that. If somehow you were doing that in insurance, you better explain how that happened.\par \par \uc2 I think I actually found that line somewhere else on the internet -- on a resume from a physicist.\par \par \uc2 -- Benjamin Taub, Dataspace\par \par \uc2 Going for a ride\par \par \uc2 This client was a law firm based in Washington, D.C., and my candidate was at the office waiting to meet the owner of the firm for an interview.\par \par \uc2 Suddenly, I got a message saying that the owner was running late and that he wouldn't have time to do the interview at the office. Apparently this was very common -- he said that when this happened, he usually talked to candidates in his car while his driver drove around the city -- like he was the Godfather or something.\par \par \uc2 So the interview took place in the back of the owner's car, and he was kind enough to offer to drop off the attorney at his home.\par \par \uc2 Afterwards, the attorney called me. He goes, "Yeah, I was standing outside the office building and the owner of the firm drove up. And he just said, 'Get in!'"\par \par \uc2 I think the lesson here is: Be prepared for anything.\par \par \uc2 -- Katherine Loanzon, Kinney Recruiting\par \par \uc2 Jaws of life\par \par \uc2 I had a candidate who flew in to town to interview at a company that was based in Tennessee. They were far enough away from the airport that they flew her in for the interview and then had a car service pick her up.\par \par \uc2 The candidate was in the car, stopped at a stoplight, when the car was rear-ended by this truck. They had to pry her out of the car using the jaws of life. Thankfully, she wasn't seriously injured, she was OK, but they took her to a local hospital.\par \par \uc2 The company got wind of the accident, and the CEO was horrified that this happened and so he beelined it over to the hospital to say how sorry he was and check if she was OK. She was so good-natured about it. And she joked, "This is the first time I've ever had a job interview from a hospital bed!"\par \par \uc2 She ended up doing a proper interview, and I think she got the job!\par \par \uc2 -- David S. Martin, Sterling Martin Associates\par \par \uc2 'What's your name again?'\par \par \uc2 I specialize in international legal recruiting and strategy, and all of my clients are law firms. In this case, my client was a fairly prominent U.S. firm that was looking to hire someone in London, and they had not been very successful at it -- there's a lot of competition among American firms searching for English lawyers.\par \par \uc2 I was finally able to present a candidate who was not just fantastic, but very well-known in his industry and quite successful. It took months to get this guy to agree to a meeting.\par \par \uc2 Finally, my client came to London for this long-awaited meeting. Almost immediately after sitting down, one of my client's partners leaned forward. He said to the candidate: "What's your name again?"\par \par \uc2 It's English to say, but I was completely gobsmacked.\par \par \uc2 In less than 10 minutes, this guy glanced at his phone and said something urgent had come up and excused himself to leave, never to be heard from again.\par \par \uc2 So the candidate left, and we were all left staring at each other. I said, "You all understand what just happened, right?" And the guy who asked what the candidate's name was, he said, "What happened? Was there a problem?"\par \par \uc2 -- Robert Bata, WarwickPlace Legal\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 These interviews have been edited and condensed for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240727ek7r00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
