\par\par \b \uc2 MANSION --- 'Til the Landslide Brought Them Down --- Jackson, Wyo., relies on nearby Idaho towns to house its workforce. A catastrophic landslide blocked the commute and upended the way towns on both side of the state line do business.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nancy Keates \par \uc2 2,490 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par M1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cassie and Mark Vandeventer were used to commuting around 45 minutes each way from their home in worker-populated Driggs, Idaho, to their jobs across the state line in pricey Jackson, Wyo. It was an easy trade-off: Living in Driggs meant they could afford a house with a yard for their dog, Cooper.\par \par \uc2 Then, on June 8, a substantial section of the Teton Pass highway collapsed in a landslide, severing a crucial connection between Jackson and the towns of Victor and Driggs, Idaho. A subsequent detour added 60 miles each way, which added three hours to the Vardeventers' round-trip commute to her job as a rehab nurse at St. John's Health and his job at a construction services company.\par \par \uc2 They weren't the only ones making the trek. About 40% of Jackson's 25,000-strong workforce commutes, with a large chunk of that coming from Idaho, thanks to Jackson's high real estate and rental costs. Cassie says her commute became suddenly not only much longer, but also dangerous -- the driving became reckless because of how tired and frustrated people were. "It just didn't work for me," she says.\par \par \uc2 St. John's offered to put up Cassie, 42, in a small room at a nearby log-cabin lodge it runs for patients and visitors. She also had the option of staying on a bunk bed in one of the dormlike sleep rooms normally used for staff on call. But their dog wasn't allowed at either place, so Cassie spent almost two weeks sleeping in Mark's office on a blow-up queen-size mattress on a concrete floor squeezed under his desk. There was no shower. "You roll around on a concrete floor on an air mattress -- it's hard to get sleep," she says.\par \par \uc2 Like many mountain resort communities, housing that could be affordable to Jackson's workforce is in very short supply. There is limited land available for building on the Wyoming side; 97% of Teton County, Wyo., land is federally protected from development. This has led to ever-increasing home prices, with the median now hovering around $4.5 million, compared with $1.6 million in 2019, according to Devon Viehman of the Jackson Hole Report. Between 2015 and 2021, the home sales price in Jackson has grown at double the rate of the increase in wages there.\par \par \uc2 The result is insufficient affordable housing in Jackson, which has led it to rely on other, less expensive towns to house its worker population. All it takes is an act of nature, such as landslides, storms, or avalanches, to instantly rearrange a commute, slicing its workforce.\par \par \uc2 In a survey of Jackson Hole Chamber of Commerce members and their workers taken a week after the road collapse, the inability to provide housing was the number one concern. There are currently 1,543 people registered as waiting for affordable housing in the Jackson area compared with 864 in 2019, according to April Norton, the director of Jackson/Teton County's Affordable Housing Department.\par \par \uc2 While a temporary detour of the crippled roadway finished in late June has substantially eased the commute time, a permanent fix isn't expected for months, causing some Idaho-based workers to rethink their choice to work in Jackson at all. As a result, Jackson-based employers are offering more flexible work arrangements and, in a first, some Jackson residents are opening their extra rooms and guesthouses to local workers on an as-needed basis, often for free.\par \par \uc2 Jodi Peterson, 52, works at \uc2 Jackson Hole Mountain Resort\uc2  in the winter and as the bar manager for a catering company in Jackson in the summer. Her husband works for \uc2 Jackson Hole Mountain Resort\uc2  year-round. They live in Driggs on the Idaho side and typically commute four to five days a week over the pass because they have a 10-year-old son. After the road collapsed, her husband started sleeping in his truck in the parking lot of the ski resort three to four nights a week and she started staying on a sofa in the kitchen of the catering company a couple nights a week.\par \par \uc2 Peterson expects they will continue camping and sleeping on a couch several nights a week until the road is permanently fixed. "It's making me recalculate my situation," she says. That includes rethinking whether she wants to commute for work at all. "I don't know if I will do that anymore," she says.\par \par \uc2 Dani Allred, 55, lives between Victor and Driggs. She owns a gardening company called High Country Bloomers with 16 clients in Wyoming and 35 clients Idaho. When the road collapsed, she cut her commute to Jackson to one day a week from two days a week, a schedule she says she will maintain even when the road is completely back to normal.\par \par \uc2 Allred says she's also considering cutting back on the number of clients in Wyoming, which she says would be financially feasible. But she says she has grown close to her clients there and doesn't want to abandon them because she knows they would have a difficult time finding a replacement. "It's hard to say 'I'm outta here' because I wouldn't know who to suggest they hire,'" she says. She hasn't taken on new clients in many years and doesn't keep a wait list. "I just say no," she says. She says the road collapse "made people in Jackson Hole see that without all of us they'd be in a world of hurt. They've realized how important we are to them."\par \par \uc2 Since the slide, companies in Jackson have been forced to come up with some creative solutions to retain their commuting workers. Jed Mixter, owner of Two Ocean Builders in Jackson, says his construction company has had to adjust its schedules. He's now offering employees four 10-hour days or three 12-hour days instead of five days a week. "We are concerned about losing them," he says. While there is still a wage difference between the Jackson and Idaho sides for construction workers, that has shrunk in recent years as demand for more high-end homes on the Idaho side has grown as prices in Jackson have soared, he says.\par \par \uc2 Dave Knaus also switched things up after the collapse. His Geyser Kayak Tours, which operates at Yellowstone Lake on the Wyoming side, has 10 guides, six of whom live in Idaho. His solution was to change the schedule from shifts every other day to one with three days on, three days off. He then moved a six-sleeper camping van he owned to a site near the park entrance and hooked it up to the internet. Now, two employees can sleep there for three nights at a time and not have to drive back to Idaho.\par \par \uc2 The change is working so well and is making his staff so much happier that it will be permanent, says Knaus. He says it is a safer alternative for his employees, who used to have to drive back over the pass at night since tours sometimes didn't end until 9 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Similar to Jackson's schools, police force and many businesses, a large number of essential workers at St. John's Health -- some 115, including its CEO -- live on the Idaho side. When the slide hit, the hospital was able to find about 32 temporary beds between the hospital sleep rooms and the lodge, which gets especially busy during the summer season. It also got special permission to temporarily let staff park campers in the administrative section of a neighboring National Elk Refuge, says spokeswoman Karen Connelly.\par \par \uc2 Also playing a role in finding housing was the Community Foundation of Jackson Hole, a nonprofit that manages philanthropy. Following the slide, it started its first housing matchmaking program: 41 people, a mixture of full-time and part-time residents, agreed to let people stay in their homes free of charge. The list spiked the first week after the collapse to 76 people who needed housing help.\par \par \uc2 Laurie Andrews, president of the organization, says she wasn't sure if the homeowners would step up. "You say you want to help but do you really mean it," she says. But she was happily surprised at the response. "There have been great connections made," she says.\par \par \uc2 Cassie Vandeventer, the rehab nurse, was matched with Mary Hayes, 70, who bought property near Jackson with her husband, Bill Hayes, in 2011 when they were living in Houston. They built a five-bedroom house with a guesthouse in 2014 and moved in full time six years ago. Hayes says she heard about the need for housing via a group text put out by a friend on the hospital's foundation board. "It wasn't even a decision. We just said yes right away," says Hayes. She believes many more homeowners would be willing to open up rooms for commuters if they knew how much it was needed.\par \par \uc2 When she drove up to Hayes's house, Cassie was in shock. "It's huge and beautiful," she says. She was also in shock about their generosity. "They opened their door to people they didn't even know, even though we had a dog. Who does that?" The guesthouse has two bedrooms, which worked especially well because she and her husband both snore. There is a full-size refrigerator, a fireplace and a babbling brook right outside. "It's peaceful and quiet," she says. They stayed there for six nights until the \uc2 Wyoming Department of Transportation\uc2  reopened the road over Teton Pass with a new detour on June 28 that returned the commute time to near normal. However, the steep, sharp curve in the new detour has many residents worried about navigating it during the winter months. Even when the road is completely rebuilt, this will do nothing to mitigate the frequent storms that make the Jackson/Idaho commute treacherous year-round.\par \par \uc2 Cassie will continue to stay in the Hayes's guesthouse periodically, especially in the winter, when ice and snow make the driving dangerous and causes closures, something Mary Hayes confirms. "I'm not one to ask for help, so how it worked out was a blessing," says Cassie. "Now that I have her number and know where she lives, I will call her if I'm in a pickle." She says she has thought about looking for a job on the Idaho side. "But that was before I met Mary," she says.\par \par \uc2 Dan Heist, 79, who retired to Jackson in 2015 to a house he built on 5 acres, offered up his guesthouse, rent-free, through the Community Foundation's matchmaking program. He ended up hosting a family of three who were sleeping in their car after the slide to avoid the long commute. Once they saw his guesthouse, they asked how long they could stay and if they could rent it permanently, he says. Heist said no, because his grandchildren come to stay there often, but he says when it is empty he would let them stay there free again. "It's easy -- here's a key. They're respectful. So why don't more people do that? It's a matter of someone facilitating it."\par \par \uc2 Paul Hansen, a retired conservationist, and his wife, Kay Stratman, an artist, also offered up their home through the Community Foundation program. A woman who works 12-hour shifts at a sandwich shop in Jackson but lives in Victor and her daughter, 19, who is working at a camp in Jackson this summer, stayed in their lofted guest bedroom with its own bathroom for five days free. They all had dinner together the first night.\par \par \uc2 Hansen says he would offer his home up again and he thinks it would be great to keep the mechanism for matchmaking in place so it can continue. He says around 20% of the houses in his neighborhood are empty much of the year.\par \par \uc2 "The universe highlighted the problem," Hansen says of the landslide. "Now we need to fix it."\par \par \uc2 Jackson's government has been seeking a fix for decades. The county of Teton, Wyo., has 1,618 affordable housing units, all built since 1994, and about 730 units in the pipeline, says Alex Norton, principal of OPS Strategies. But according to the 2022 Teton Region Housing Needs Assessment report, put out by Teton County, Wyo., and Teton County, Idaho, jobs have been growing at more than twice the rate of housing units in the area since 2010. The report estimates the town needs to create at least 2,000 new below-market homes in the next few years just to maintain its current 60% resident workforce. Behind the demand is continued local job growth and expected retirements (estimated to be 13% of the 2022 local workforce), both of which bring new workers to Jackson and Teton County.\par \par \uc2 Some are skeptical that things will suddenly get better now. "There's been a lot of progress since the 1990s, but it's still a problem and it's getting worse with time," says Anne Cresswell, executive director of the Jackson Hole Community Housing Trust. She's not optimistic that there will be significant change. "My concern is everything will just go back to normal when the pass is fixed," she says. "We have short memories and we will forget about this."\par \par \uc2 One thing that might not go back to normal, however, is the businesses and opportunities for the towns on the Idaho side.\par \par \uc2 Dave Knaus from Geyser Kayak Tours says he's seen a new desire for people to work and vacation in Idaho. Knaus says more outfitters are setting up shop on the Idaho side because Jackson is getting too expensive for guests to stay and because the activities accessed from the Idaho side are just as good as those from the Wyoming side. "Once someone finds a sustainable income on the Idaho side, you never see them in Jackson anymore. There's no need," he says.\par \par \uc2 Brice Nelson has owned the Knotty Pine Supper Club, a restaurant in Victor, for 29 years. After the slide, Nelson put out an ad for a new cook; instead of the usual one or two applicants who tended to be unqualified, he received 30 applications immediately and deleted the ad. He hired a chef who lives in Idaho. She had been working at a fine-dining restaurant in Jackson and decided she no longer wanted to commute.\par \par \uc2 "So many people have had their lives upended," said Nelson. "They realize they didn't move to the mountains to spend their time commuting."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240726ek7q00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
