\par\par \b \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2  Get Big Win in California --- State's top court rejects an effort to classify gig workers as employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By Preetika Rana \par \uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Uber Technologies\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and other companies that depend on gig workers scored a victory with California's top court, affirming their independent-contractor model in the state, a decision that caps a yearslong legal battle over how their drivers should be classified.\par \par \uc2 California's Supreme Court upheld a lower-court ruling that said Proposition 22, a 2020 ballot measure that allowed the companies to continue classifying their drivers as gig workers, was constitutional. Some ride-share drivers and a labor union had challenged that decision, leading to a prolonged legal fight that wound up in the state's highest court.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  and others are in a global tug of war with regulators over whether and how to grant more benefits such as paid sick leave and health insurance to workers in the so-called gig economy, where apps distribute individual tasks to a pool of people whom companies generally regard as independent contractors.\par \par \uc2 California sued \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  in 2020, saying they were in violation of a new state law that sought to reclassify their drivers as employees who would be eligible for such benefits.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and gig-labor-dependent companies \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  and \uc2 Instacart\uc2  proposed a ballot measure to let state voters decide whether the law should apply to them. They promised workers flexibility alongside some benefits if the ballot measure passed. State voters passed it with an overwhelming majority.\par \par \uc2 Under Proposition 22, the companies offer a minimum earnings guarantee, health insurance for drivers who work 15 hours or more a week, occupational accident insurance coverage and other protections. Critics say that the benefits fall short of those awarded to full-time employees.\par \par \uc2 "Whether drivers or couriers choose to earn just a few hours a week or more, their freedom to work when and how they want is now firmly etched into California law, putting an end to misguided attempts to force them into an employment model that they overwhelmingly do not want," \uc2 Uber\uc2  said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  called the decision a "huge victory," adding that it was relieved that the "unnecessary legal process is behind us."\par \par \uc2 The California arm of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , which challenged the measure, said it was disappointed. We "won't stop fighting to win greater workplace rights and protections on the job," said Tia Orr, the executive director of the union in California.\par \par \uc2 Proposition 22's successful passage set the tone for gig-worker regulation in the rest of the country. Washington state later passed a law preserving the companies' independent-contractor models. The companies reached a settlement with the attorney general of Massachusetts last month that promised workers a minimum wage alongside some benefits, mirroring Proposition 22.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's decision was being closely watched by analysts concerned that an unfavorable ruling could radically alter the companies' business models as they had just turned a corner on profits.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2 , which has a large global footprint, has had to make bigger concessions outside the U.S. It agreed to grant its U.K. drivers an employment status entitling them to vacation pay and pension contributions after exhausting its legal options in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240726ek7q0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
