\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Police Hustle For Recruits With Billboards --- Departments get creative as a hot job market makes it hard to get and keep cops\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry \par \uc2 808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Officer Andrae Smith, the recruiter at the Plano Police Department, had jobs to fill. So he ordered up five billboards -- one in Norman, Okla., and the rest in Texas -- with a big, bold number: $97,646.\par \par \uc2 That was the top salary a police officer could earn in the wealthy Dallas suburb of Plano. Since last year, when the billboards went up, budget increases let the department bump up its pay again. That number is now $108,150, and the billboards have been updated, too.\par \par \uc2 "The No. 1 attraction is the pay," said Smith, who got the billboard idea after hearing about another police department that tried something similar.\par \par \uc2 It is an expensive endeavor, about $30,000 per billboard a year, but it was necessary when a tight labor market made it hard to find employees. The five billboards have so far attracted 386 applicants to take Plano's police exam, resulting in 30 hires.\par \par \uc2 "Our goal was to pull at the shirttail of every individual who had interest in being in law enforcement," Smith said.\par \par \uc2 Policing has struggled to get and keep workers since the pandemic. Burned-out employees have resigned or retired. There were nearly 19,000 fewer officers in 2023 than 2019, a 3% drop, according to Labor Department data.\par \par \uc2 Hiring can be especially hard for departments in or around big cities. Usually, officers tended to progress from police departments in small and midsize cities to large ones, said Chuck Wexler, executive director of the Police Executive Research Forum.\par \par \uc2 Now, many want the opposite, especially in the years since a police officer killed \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in Minneapolis in 2020. Small and midsize agencies have more sworn officers than they did before the pandemic, according to the nonprofit's survey last year of more than 200 police departments. Big agencies have fewer officers than they used to.\par \par \uc2 According to Smith, marketing Plano's bumper wages is working. The department had 14 positions available the day it launched its first billboard, on its staff of more than 400 officers. Today it has eight.\par \par \uc2 Officer Karina Hinojosa transferred to Plano from the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2  in early 2017. Plano offered her six months of paid training, and the lower cost of living and good schools for her daughter helped clinch the deal.\par \par \uc2 "For me, it was quality of life," Hinojosa said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, cities have increased perks to attract candidates. This year, the Bellevue Police Department in Washington state began offering take-home cars as a recruitment and retention tool. It also offers the option to use up to three on-duty hours a week to work out.\par \par \uc2 "For me, it's really important for you to find the right organization that has the right culture," said Wendell Shirley, the police chief. He said Bellevue received more than 750 applications last year and hired 33 officers.\par \par \uc2 Other states are easing restrictions on joining the police. Last month, Dallas relaxed its policy on visible tattoos. Last year, Pennsylvania said it would remove a requirement that state trooper recruits have a certain number of college credits. The Seattle Police Department recently began accepting applications from recipients of the Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals program. Typically, only U.S. citizens can join the police.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, the average annual wage for police and sheriff's patrol officers was $76,550, about a 13% increase from 2019, according to Labor Department data. The average wage for all occupations was $65,470 in 2023, though that was up 22% in the same time frame.\par \par \uc2 Some of the forces that dented the supply of police recruits in recent years are abating. Though the job market remains historically strong, it has started to cool, making it less appealing to look for another job. Violent crime rates have also fallen sharply.\par \par \uc2 More sworn officers were hired in 2023 than in any of the previous four years, according to the Police Executive Research Forum's survey. Resignations were still up compared with before the pandemic, but they dropped compared with 2022.\par \par \uc2 The Laramie County Sheriff's Office in Wyoming, for its part, on social media and on a billboard encourage people to come work in a state where "breaking the law is still illegal."\par \par \uc2 Laramie County Sheriff Brian Kozak said he has received over 200 applications since May 1, compared with 144 in the first four months of the year.\par \par \uc2 Evan Storch, 33, was a police sergeant in Arizona when he saw recruitment videos from the Laramie County Sheriff's Office on social media. He now earns less than he did in Arizona, but his housing costs are lower in Wyoming and he recently earned a promotion to lieutenant.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich308\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4620
