\par\par \b \uc2 World News: \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  Opens Probe Into Workplace Culture --- Trustees conduct review as corporate partners ask about WSJ investigation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shalini Ramachandran, Jenny Strasburg and Suzanne Vranica \par \uc2 518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2 's board is working with a global law firm to investigate its workplace culture, weeks after The Wall Street Journal revealed allegations of harassment and discrimination at the organization behind the elite Davos gatherings.\par \par \uc2 The board of trustees has formed a special committee and tapped \uc2 Thomas Buberl\uc2 , chief executive of French insurer \uc2 AXA\uc2 , to oversee the inquiry, said people familiar with the discussions. The trustees are being supported by \uc2 Covington & Burling\uc2 , a law firm that has handled other high-profile workplace reviews.\par \par \uc2 Based on interviews with more than 80 current and former employees, the Journal reported on June 29 that the forum has allowed an atmosphere hostile to women and Black people to fester.\par \par \uc2 Some allege sexual harassment, pregnancy-related discrimination and racial discrimination. The forum disputed the Journal's characterization of events and said the organization has zero tolerance for harassment or discrimination and has responded appropriately to any complaints received.\par \par \uc2 After the article's publication, several of the forum's corporate partners contacted the organization to ask about the allegations raised in the article, including \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , \uc2 Mastercard\uc2 , \uc2 Merck & Co.\uc2 , Danish manufacturer Velux, design consultancy \uc2 Arup\uc2  and global property-management giant \uc2 Jones Lang LaSalle\uc2 , according to people familiar with the outreaches.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Friday, the forum told the Journal that independent members of the board of trustees will oversee a review with the support of outside counsel.\par \par \uc2 "The board committee will report back to the Board of Trustees once the review is completed," the forum said.\par \par \uc2 The forum's board of trustees has been led for more than five decades by the organization's founder, Klaus Schwab.\par \par \uc2 The board consists of about 30 members from various sectors, including the chief executives of \uc2 Salesforce\uc2  and \uc2 Accenture\uc2 , as well as Queen Rania Al Abdullah of Jordan and the heads of the World Bank and International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 In meetings of the trustees called specifically to discuss issues raised in the Journal's reporting, some trustees with experience running public companies argued more strongly for an independent investigation, one of the people said, while others said the forum should keep the matter internal until they learned more.\par \par \uc2 People close to the board said the spotlight on the forum's internal culture has also complicated the discussion around who would succeed Schwab, who announced in May that he was planning to step aside as executive chairman and stay on as nonexecutive chairman of the board of trustees.\par \par \uc2 Some trustees have privately expressed concerns that issues highlighted by the Journal's reporting, including the potential impact on support from partner companies and organizations that attend forum events, could scare off potential candidates to lead the forum, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 In an internal email to staff on July 5, \uc2 WEF\uc2  President \uc2 Borge Brende\uc2  wrote that he had heard from many employees in the intervening days and "while we are steadfast in our commitment to do better, we can always improve."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240720ek7k0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
