\par\par \b \uc2 An Enemy of the Workingman at the \uc2 RNC\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Saltsman and Charlyce Bozzello \par \uc2 535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  President Sean O'Brien gave a fiery speech denouncing corporate America. He alleged that big business has "sold out" workers and declared that labor unions are needed to secure "real improvements" on the job.\par \par \uc2 The union leader's speech was predictable. The setting -- a prime-time slot at the Republican National Convention -- was unprecedented. Mr. O'Brien hasn't endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but his presence at the convention is telling. Mr. O'Brien apparently believes he can persuade Republicans to support organized labor's vision. He may be right. But if he succeeds, workers would be the losers.\par \par \uc2 Start with Mr. O'Brien's support for eliminating right-to-work laws -- laws he derides as a "con game." In 26 states, it is currently illegal to make the payment of union dues a condition of employment. Employees in those states don't have to pay dues to a union for fear of getting fired. Mr. O'Brien would take that choice away.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Brien appears not to care much about what workers want. Take his strong support of the Biden administration's crackdown on independent contractors. He would have these independent workers forced into W-2 employment so that it would be "easier" for them to "organize and join unions."\par \par \uc2 Millions of independent workers have no interest in being full-time employees -- or union members. Mr. O'Brien ignores numerous polls and countless testimonials from freelancers who would prefer to stay independent.\par \par \uc2 The least defensible "reform" Mr. O'Brien supports is the effective elimination of the secret ballot for workplace organizing. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  endorsed 2023 legislation that could severely limit this right. Rather than provide employees with the privacy to make their choice on workplace representation, Mr. O'Brien would force them to declare their choice publicly. Anyone interested in fair elections should shudder at this violation of workplace-election integrity.\par \par \uc2 In his \uc2 RNC\uc2  speech, Mr. O'Brien dropped the names of several conservative senators, including vice-presidential nominee J.D. Vance, who he believes will support his agenda. But it's a senator Mr. O'Brien overlooked who will actually help workers: Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 The Employee Rights Act, which Mr. Scott introduced in 2022, aimed to protect secret-ballot votes and allow union members to opt out of sending their dues to causes they don't support. Mr. O'Brien's members would probably like a say in where their money goes. While nearly 40% of \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  plan to vote for Mr. Trump this year, 99% of the union's advocacy spending goes to left-wing organizations.\par \par \uc2 Mr. O'Brien has moved away from what his workers actually want. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , like most unions, are ultimately loyal to their own bottom line. If Republicans are serious about standing up for working people, union bosses like Sean O'Brien aren't the kind of company they should keep.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Saltsman is executive director of the Employment Policies Institute. Ms. Bozzello is communications director of the Center for Union Facts.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Sean O'Brien Paid a Price for His GOP Speech" -- WSJ July 27, 2024)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240719ek7j0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
