\par\par \uc2 Technology & Media\par \b \uc2 Report Cites Injuries on Prime Day --- Probe found that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's injury rate jumped during the shopping bonanza\b0\par\par\uc2 By Victoria Albert \par \uc2 679 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 's Prime Day sales event, a shopping bonanza for customers, has been "a major cause of injuries" for warehouse workers, a Senate probe found.\par \par \uc2 The report, released by Sen. Bernie Sanders, (I., Vt.), found that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's injury rate jumped during Prime Day and the holiday season. The report, which followed a yearlong investigation, cited internal data that the e-commerce company provided from its Prime Day in 2019. This year's Prime Day, a two-day event, kicked off Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The report accused \uc2 Amazon\uc2  of prioritizing speed over safety, saying it pressured employees to work long hours and shirked some safety procedures. "This is not an acceptable set of practices from one of the richest companies in the world," the report said. "\uc2 Amazon\uc2  must address its injury crisis and ensure that all workers are safe at their jobs, especially during the most intense and demanding periods."\par \par \uc2 The interim report relied on information gathered during the investigation by the Senate Committee on Health, Education, Labor and Pensions. Sanders, the committee's chairman, has long been a vocal critic of the company.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , one of the most dominant forces in the e-commerce market, has for years been the subject of federal and state scrutiny over the treatment of the workers at its facilities. An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  representative said Tuesday employee safety is the company's top priority and accused the Senate committee of painting a false, one-sided picture of the company's work conditions that ignores progress it has made in recent years.\par \par \uc2 "If someone wants to truly understand the facts about our safety record and our progress toward being the safest company in the industries in which we operate, we encourage them to review our annual safety report or come visit one of our fulfillment sites to see for themselves," the spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 In a post on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's 2023 safety data released earlier this year, the company said it has made substantial improvements to worker safety in the past four years.\par \par \uc2 Prime Day, during which the e-commerce giant slashes prices on a variety of products for its Prime members, provides a major boost for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  during the typically slower summer months. Customers spent $12.7 billion in the U.S. during the two-day event last year and are expected to spend more than $14 billion this year, according to Adobe Analytics.\par \par \uc2 Prime membership costs $139 a year. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said last month that it has more than 200 million paid users.\par \par \uc2 During Prime Day 2019, the Senate report found that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's rate of recordable injuries -- those severe enough that they required more than first aid -- was more than double the industry average. The total rate, which includes more minor injuries, was nearly 45 injuries per 100 workers, the report said.\par \par \uc2 The report also accused \uc2 Amazon\uc2  of pressuring workers not to seek outside medical care for their injuries to lower its recordable injury rate and underreporting its count of serious injuries.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said claims that it underreports injuries are false. The spokesperson also said the company has reduced its recordable incident rate by 28% since 2019, and has reduced its lost time incident rate -- which includes injuries that require an employee to miss at least a day of work -- by 75% over the same period.\par \par \uc2 The Senate report said \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's recent investments in safety represented a fraction of the company's profits, and that it has more work to do to keep workers safe. It said it would release additional information in a future report.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has a market capitalization of around $2 trillion. Last month, it became the fifth U.S. company to cross the $2 trillion milestone, joining other tech companies including \uc2 Google\uc2  parent \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 . For Prime Day this year, the company recruited celebrities and influencers such as Millie Bobby Brown and Alix Earle, and teamed up with rapper Megan Thee Stallion on a song.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240717ek7h0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
