\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- U.S. HR Lobby Pares DEI Label\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ray A. Smith and Lynn Cook \par \uc2 498 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The country's top organization for human-resources professionals is distancing itself from the "equity" plank of diversity, equity and inclusion, or DEI.\par \par \uc2 SHRM\uc2 , a lobbying-and-advocacy group with 340,000 members, announced this week that it wants employers to focus on inclusion and diversity efforts -- in that order. The group, a powerful lobby in Washington, said that it is moving away from equity language to ensure no group of workers appears to get preferential treatment. Equity, in HR parlance, is the notion that companies should take steps to level the playing field for workers.\par \par \uc2 "By emphasizing inclusion-first, we aim to address the current shortcomings of DE&I programs, which have led to societal backlash," the group said in a statement posted on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The announcement is the latest example of business leaders grappling with diversity measures during a time when those very efforts face fire from legal, political and employee groups. Diversity recruiting efforts are shrinking at some big banks and consulting firms, and elsewhere companies are reducing staff or shifting initiatives once designed to advance underrepresented minorities.\par \par \uc2 A few years ago \uc2 SHRM\uc2  stopped using the DEI acronym in favor of inclusion, equity and diversity, or IE&D. The group emphasizes inclusion so that all employees, including white men, feel they belong, according to \uc2 Johnny C. Taylor Jr.\uc2 , president of \uc2 SHRM\uc2 . The decision to drop "equity" and create a new acronym -- I&D -- is a move to temper political polarization around diversity, he added. "We need a world where inclusion is front and center," he wrote. "Everyone has a right to feel that they belong in the workplace."\par \par \uc2 SHRM\uc2 's \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  post was quickly flooded with comments, many critical. Hundreds of people accused \uc2 SHRM\uc2  of backing away from DEI efforts and several said they would cancel their memberships or join other HR groups.\par \par \uc2 Zachary Temple, a senior HR executive at \uc2 KeyBank\uc2  in Cleveland, accused the group of "kowtowing to political adversity," he wrote on \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 . "Inclusion does not guarantee equity. As others have rightfully called out, it is easy to see through this 'rebranding' for what it actually is."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Taylor said the group's critics may have missed the point. "We are not at all indicating that equity doesn't matter. We are saying that the full definition of inclusion must encompass equity," he said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Corporate efforts around DEI have typically defined equality as treating each person the same. Equity, on the other hand, considers that not everyone starts from the same sociopolitical point and makes adjustments to even the playing field. A 2022 Gallup survey found fewer than 3 in 10 employees strongly agreed that their employer was fair to everyone.\par \par \uc2 Last year's Supreme Court decision striking down affirmative action in college admissions seemingly removed the legal rationale buttressing many diversity initiatives.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Lauren Weber contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240713ek7d00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
