\par\par \b \uc2 OECD\uc2  Expects Jobs Markets To Cool\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Hannon \par \uc2 675 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unemployment rates are set to pick up only slightly across the world's rich countries in the short term, while real wages will continue to rise as profit growth cools, the \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2  said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In its annual report on the jobs market, the Paris-based policy advisory body said wages have been rising faster than prices during the past year, but real wages remain below their levels from late 2019 in a number of countries, including the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OECD\uc2  said there are signs that the jobs market is cooling, with the number of vacancies falling relative to the number of people looking for work. But it doesn't expect to see the sharp rise in jobless rates that have accompanied past periods in which central banks have raised their key interest rates to cool inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The labor market remains pretty strong," said \uc2 Stefano Scarpetta\uc2 , the \uc2 OECD\uc2 's director for employment. "The labor market is easing, but slowly."\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the \uc2 OECD\uc2  expects employment to increase by less than 1% in both 2024 and 2025, with the unemployment rate remaining around 4%.\par \par \uc2 That is broadly in line with the outlook across the \uc2 OECD\uc2 's 38 members, which are mostly rich countries. The \uc2 OECD\uc2  forecast that employment will grow by 0.7% this year and next, having increased by 1.7% in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Workers suffered a decline in their real wages during the surge in consumer prices that began in early 2021. The \uc2 OECD\uc2  said that during the year through the first quarter of 2024, real wages were rising again as inflation cooled. Out of the 35 countries for which data was available, 29 recorded a rise in real wage. Among those that didn't were France and Japan.\par \par \uc2 On average, real wages were 3.5% higher than a year earlier, a development that should support consumer spending and economic growth. However, real wages were still below their 2019 levels in 16 countries, including the U.S., where the shortfall stood at 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OECD\uc2  expects the recovery in real wages to continue this year. Offsetting that upward pressure on prices, profit growth has slowed in most countries. While profits grew much more rapidly than wages in 2021, the \uc2 OECD\uc2  estimates that since the start of 2022, labor costs grew more rapidly than profits in about two-thirds of the countries with data available.\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 OECD\uc2 's view, a squeeze on profits can allow for further wage rises without triggering a fresh pickup in inflation. That is an outcome that central banks have feared since the start of the inflation surge.\par \par \uc2 "There are no signs of a price-wage spiral," the \uc2 OECD\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 However, it warned that wage rises could yet have an impact on inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Looking ahead, it will continue to be important to strike a balance between allowing wages to make up some of the ground they have lost in terms of purchasing power and limiting further inflationary pressures," the \uc2 OECD\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The recovery of the job market from the initial blow delivered by the spread of the Covid-19 virus has been particularly strong for workers in lower-wage parts of the economy, and for women, the \uc2 OECD\uc2  said. In 17 of the 33 countries with available data, traditionally lower-pay industries recorded a faster rise in real wages between 2019 and 2023, while employment growth for women has outpaced that of men over the same period.\par \par \uc2 "Wages are performing better in the lower end than in the middle or high end," Scarpetta said.\par \par \uc2 Looking forward, the \uc2 OECD\uc2  said the transition to jobs that produce lower greenhouse gas emissions could have big regional impacts, with many of the new jobs that are created being in different locations to those that are lost. The \uc2 OECD\uc2  estimates that just 7% of employment is in what it describes as high-emission industries, but those who lose their jobs might face a lengthy period of lower earnings without retraining.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw512\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7680\pichgoal7200
