\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Government to the Jobs Rescue\b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department's jobs report on Friday showed employers in June continued to hire at a healthy rate. So why do many Americans worry about the labor market?\par \par \uc2 Look under the hood, and most new jobs being created in recent months are in trades that rely on government spending.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 206,000 jobs in June, though the unemployment rate ticked up 0.1 percentage point to 4.1% owing to an increase in the labor force. The good news is that labor force participation among prime working-age Americans is the highest in more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the jobs market appears to be slowing. New jobs were revised down by a combined 111,000 for May and April. Job growth has averaged about 177,000 over the past three months, compared to 220,000 for the past year.\par \par \uc2 Nearly three-quarters of the net new jobs in June were in government, healthcare and social assistance. These industries also made up roughly half of the new jobs in May and more than 90% in April. Construction accounted for a large share of the rest. Nurses, social workers and plumbers are still in high demand, but less so others.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing employment has been flat since November. Information has shed jobs over the past two years. Job growth in leisure and hospitality has slowed to a trickle. Total hours worked in leisure and hospitality haven't budged since late last year because employers are cutting hours. Could rising minimum wages in many states be one of the reasons? It probably is in California.\par \par \uc2 Falling employment over the last six months among young people ages 20 to 24 also bears watching. None of this screams recession, but the report does indicate that government spending is increasingly driving job growth. The U.S. is running $2 trillion deficits, which aren't sustainable. How long can government sustain an economy before something cracks? We may find out.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240706ek760001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
