\par\par \b \uc2 Jobs Data Bolster Case for September Rate Cut --- Labor market beat expectations in June, but there are signs of gradual cooling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart and Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 1,093 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. added a solid 206,000 jobs last month, the Labor Department reported on Friday, slightly beating expectations and continuing a remarkably strong run. But the unemployment rate ticked up to 4.1%, a sign of slack in a labor market that has already shown some hints of gradually slowing down.\par \par \uc2 There were other indications as well that the job market is continuing to cool. Average hourly earnings were up 3.9% in June from a year earlier, marking their smallest gain since 2021. The counts for both April and May were revised lower by a combined 111,000 jobs. The labor-force participation rate, the share of working-age people who were employed or seeking work, ticked up to 62.6% -- an indication that more people entered the labor market.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rose, with the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq Composite hitting records. Treasury yields declined.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report seems to have affirmed investors' view that the economy is slowing, but not in a drastic way that would prompt more aggressive rate cuts. For President Biden's re-election campaign, the continued job growth counts as a positive, but the rise in the unemployment rate robs it of a valuable talking point. The June report marked the first time since 2021 that the unemployment rate clocked in above 4%.\par \par \uc2 For the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , Friday's report provides further evidence that the labor market appears to have come into better balance. A hot labor market makes it more difficult to lower rates, while the central bank is also mandated to keep the job market as strong as possible without triggering inflation.\par \par \uc2 There likely wasn't anything alarming enough in Friday's payroll report to lead Fed officials to push for a July rate cut. But eventually, some Fed officials will say that the risks of a further weakening in the labor market aren't desirable, even as others may argue that inflation still isn't showing enough progress to cut rates.\par \par \uc2 While July may be too early for a rate cut, a mild inflation report next week could lead Fed doves who are more worried about labor-market weakness to argue that it is time for the central bank to tee up a September rate cut.\par \par \uc2 One risk is that experience has shown the labor market can go from strong to weak in short order. While the unemployment rate of 4.1% is still historically low, it is up from 3.4% early last year.\par \par \uc2 The Sahm rule, a rule of thumb popularized by economist \uc2 Claudia Sahm\uc2 , says that if the average of the unemployment rate over three months rises a half-percentage point or more above the lowest the three-month average went over the previous year, the economy is in a recession. Over the past three months, the unemployment rate has averaged 4% -- 0.4 percentage point above the three-month average low of 3.6% over the past year.\par \par \uc2 Hotter inflation at the start of the year derailed a possible June rate cut and has made risk-management calculations more fraught. "I don't want to pull up too short or too early and then have inflation stay high. That's a terrible, terrible situation. I refuse to do that," \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  said in a recent interview. At the same time, officials "can't just say for sure that because the labor market is strong today, it's going to be strong tomorrow," she said.\par \par \uc2 One lesson of the Fed's belated start to raising interest rates two years ago -- when officials incorrectly judged price increases would subside rapidly -- is that "when you're too confident that your view is correct, you're prone to mistakes," she said. Moreover, the Fed was able to fix its mistake two years ago by raising rates rapidly. "If you're behind on [cutting rates] when the labor market starts to falter, it's really challenging to get that back on track. It is just not the same thing," said Daly.\par \par \uc2 High interest rates take time to fully work their way into the economy, but lately they have more clearly damped activity. Even though wages have been rising faster than prices, growth in consumer spending has slowed.\par \par \uc2 If consumers cut back too much, employers could respond by cutting staff, turning what has been a virtuous cycle of rising employment and rising spending into a vicious one.\par \par \uc2 Complicating the Fed's job, continuing pandemic-related distortions and the recent wave of immigration have made interpreting labor-market signals more difficult.\par \par \uc2 Job growth over the past year has been driven by three sectors: Healthcare, government, and leisure and hospitality. Together, they have accounted for 1.7 million of the 2.7 million jobs the economy has added over the past year. Much of that outsize growth is a matter of catching up, since all three sectors were much slower to get back to their prepandemic job levels than other employers.\par \par \uc2 Employment in all three sectors still appears low relative to their prepandemic job-growth trends. But it is unclear at what level demand for workers will be satiated, and once it is, overall job growth could slow markedly.\par \par \uc2 Nor is it clear what the right level of job growth is to keep the labor market in balance. Before the pandemic, economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  reckoned that, as a result of slowing population growth and more Americans hitting retirement age, the economy only needed to add around 70,000 to 80,000 jobs a month to keep the unemployment rate steady.\par \par \uc2 Now, as a result of the jump in immigration, they think it could be around 200,000. "But that is with very little confidence," said Goldman's chief economist, \uc2 Jan Hatzius\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Estimating how many people have come into the country is difficult. Moreover, while border crossings have fallen this year, there are likely still a large number of undocumented immigrants seeking asylum who are awaiting authorization from the government to be able to work.\par \par \uc2 Given these difficulties, Hatzius thinks the best thing to do is watch the unemployment rate, which measures the share of people working or seeking work who are unemployed. If it keeps turning higher in the months ahead, it will be an indication that the labor market has moved from a point of balance to one of deterioration.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich578\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal8670
