\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: President's Union Support Shows Signs of Slipping --- Labor backing that aided Biden in 2020 is eroding in key states, polls show\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan \par \uc2 723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden, fighting for his political life, has always seen unions as a bedrock of support. He calls himself the most pro-union president in history, with a record ranging from shoring up pension plans to prioritizing union labor in his infrastructure and clean-energy laws. In 2023, wearing a baseball cap and carrying a bullhorn, he became the first sitting president to walk a picket line, with striking auto workers outside Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Such a record ought to be paying political dividends this year in places such as Michigan and Pennsylvania, the most heavily unionized battleground states. Yet even before Biden's disastrous presidential debate performance June 27, the payoff was looking elusive: Many of the intended beneficiaries of Biden's pro-union drive show more affinity for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The challenge has only grown since the debate, which brought calls for Biden, 81 years old, to withdraw from the race. But it is far from clear a different nominee would resolve Democrats' challenges with union members. That is because, for many of these voters, the issue extends well beyond Biden and his age.\par \par \uc2 Donald Marshall, 50, has worked at a Ford factory in the Detroit area for 28 years. A member of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , he was apolitical for most of his adult life -- until Trump ran for office in 2016.\par \par \uc2 "I knew the Democrats had left working-class people in the 90s," Marshall said, citing the Clinton administration's promotion of free trade. "Trump's no genius. He just realized what was going on in the country and had the courage to stand up to these people."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's endorsement of Biden in January didn't change Marshall's mind. Nor did Biden's appearance in September on the \uc2 UAW\uc2  picket line in nearby Belleville, or the blockbuster contract that auto workers won after that.\par \par \uc2 Polls suggest that while on net unionized voters favor Biden, the advantage is slipping. Two polls by Emerson College in April and June found that union households in three critical swing states -- Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin -- favored Biden over Trump by 5 percentage points, 49% to 44%. Nationwide, in 2020, Biden carried such households by a 14-point margin, according to exit polling.\par \par \uc2 For decades, Democrats relied on unionized factory workers in industrial states to win elections. Union households' preference for Biden helped him flip Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin back into Democratic hands in 2020 after Trump carried them in 2016.\par \par \uc2 But long-term trends have undercut the strategy. Union membership fell to 10% of the workforce last year from more than 30% in the 1950s. Manufacturing, which includes union-heavy industries such as autos and steel, fell to 8% of the workforce from 39% in 1943.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a schism has opened between the Democratic Party and working-class communities from which many rank-and-file union members are drawn. In 2012, 51% of voters without a college degree cast their ballots for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . Four years later, 43% voted for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . Biden won 47% of this group in 2020, but support has slipped to 37% versus the 54% who would vote for Trump in a head-to-head race, according to a June 20-25 poll of likely voters by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College.\par \par \uc2 Part of the trend is driven by economic issues, such as Democrats' past embrace of free trade, which Biden has largely abandoned. Part of it is driven by cultural issues -- guns, gender and race -- that divide the urban, college-educated liberals who dominate the Democratic Party from noncollege workers in the suburbs and rural areas.\par \par \uc2 One Trump talking point that has resonated with many auto workers is the accusation that Biden's promotion of electric vehicles, through subsidies and emissions regulations, will kill jobs. Auto workers fear that EVs require fewer parts and less labor.\par \par \uc2 Brian Pannebecker, a retired Ford employee, runs a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group called Auto Workers For Trump 2024 with nearly 1,100 members. Most workers don't acknowledge the group, he said, but of those who do, he estimates 80-90% respond with a thumbs-up or a toot of the horn.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240706ek760001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
