\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Proposed Rules Aim to Protect Workers From Extreme Heat\b0\par\par\uc2 By H. Claire Brown \par \uc2 511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration on Tuesday released long-anticipated proposed rules that would establish the first federal safety standard for protecting workers from extreme heat on the job.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department rules, which apply to indoor and outdoor workplaces, would require employers to provide water when the heat index climbs above 80 degrees Fahrenheit. Employers also would need to provide access to shade or air-conditioned break rooms.\par \par \uc2 If the heat index surpasses 90 degrees, employers would need to offer 15-minute paid rest breaks every two hours. Companies would have to develop plans to prevent heat-related injuries.\par \par \uc2 "Across the country, workers suffer heat stroke or even die just doing their jobs," President Biden said in a speech on Tuesday. "This new rule will substantially reduce heat injuries, illnesses, and deaths for over 36 million workers to whom it will apply, from farmworkers to construction workers, postal workers, manufacturing workers, and so much more."\par \par \uc2 According to government estimates, heat exposure killed on average 33 workers a year between 1992 and 2021. That may be an undercount, according to the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 OSHA\uc2  shared the results of an investigation into a case where a 41-year-old worker collapsed while picking oranges in Florida and subsequently died. The agency found two labor violations and said the death could have been prevented.\par \par \uc2 Under the proposed rules, employers would have to set up a system to monitor symptoms of heat stroke in very hot weather. In the Florida case, that might have meant someone recognized the signs when the worker appeared confused and had difficulty speaking before collapsing, according to \uc2 OSHA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Protecting workers from heat illness and death is not rocket science," said Debbie Berkowitz, a former \uc2 OSHA\uc2  official. "This is a common-sense standard proposal . . . it will go a long way toward protecting workers from heat-related illness and death."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  has found that heat wave frequency, duration, and intensity increased between 1961 and 2023. The \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2  has predicted the average American will see 27 to 50 days per year where the temperature hits 90 degrees by 2050.\par \par \uc2 A handful of states including California, Colorado, Oregon, Washington and Minnesota have implemented their own heat-related workplace rules. Florida and Texas have laws prohibiting local governments from passing their own heat-related protections. If implemented, the federal rule would set a minimum nationwide standard.\par \par \uc2 An initial economic impact analysis by \uc2 OSHA\uc2  found the proposed federal regulation would cost businesses an average of $3,085 per establishment per year. Businesses with the highest number of affected employees include commercial kitchens, construction and manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 The proposed rule will be subject to a public comment period, and \uc2 OSHA\uc2  is required to review and address comments before publishing a final rule. If the rule is implemented, opponents have indicated they plan to challenge it in court, and it may be discarded if the White House changes hands after the election.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240703ek730000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
