\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Minimum Wage Is Set for State's \uc2 Uber\uc2 , Lyft Drivers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Preetika Rana \par \uc2 409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Uber Technologies\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  agreed to pay ride-share drivers in Massachusetts a minimum wage and give them other benefits while continuing to classify them as independent contractors, the latest development in the companies' nationwide fight to defend their gig-worker models.\par \par \uc2 The agreement, reached with the state attorney general's office, caps a yearslong legal battle over how \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers are classified in the state. Under the settlement, drivers will receive guaranteed minimum earnings of $32.50 per active hour spent on a trip as well as some benefits, including paid sick leave based on the number of hours they spend on the job.\par \par \uc2 "For years, these companies have underpaid their drivers and denied them basic benefits. Today's agreement holds \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  accountable," Massachusetts Attorney General \uc2 Andrea Joy Campbell\uc2  said. Campbell said \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  would also pay the state a combined total of $175 million to resolve allegations that they violated its wage and hour laws. Most of that money will be distributed to drivers.\par \par \uc2 "This agreement is an example of what independent, flexible work with dignity should look like," \uc2 Uber\uc2 's chief legal officer, Tony West, said. The deal comes after several U.S. states -- from Washington to Minnesota -- have instituted minimum-wage laws for ride-hailing app drivers. Other places have also tried to raise the minimum wage for food-delivery couriers. New pay increases for delivery couriers in Seattle and New York City led to higher fees by the companies on orders and lower demand.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts sued \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  in 2020, saying that their drivers should be classified as employees and entitled to a minimum wage and other benefits such as overtime pay and sick leave. In response, the companies said they would back a ballot measure to let state voters decide.\par \par \uc2 This deal settles the 2020 lawsuit, and the companies said they would no longer push for a ballot measure on the issue this year. The new Massachusetts benefits are similar to benefits the companies provide drivers in California, where a successful state ballot initiative set the tone on gig-worker legislature across the country.\par \par \uc2 "We're thrilled to reach an agreement that works for everyone, and builds on similar progress we've made in states like New York, California, Minnesota, and Washington," Jeremy Bird, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's executive vice president of driver experience, said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240628ek6s0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
