\par\par \b \uc2 Covid Retirement Wave Is Possibly With Us for Good\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison \par \uc2 465 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  has repeatedly pointed to the strong recovery in labor-force participation as a big reason why wage growth is slowing, easing pressure on inflation.\par \par \uc2 But the labor force today looks different in one crucial point than it did before the pandemic: Workers have no interest in working into their 60s or 70s.\par \par \uc2 In a March \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  survey, just 45.8% of respondents under age 62 said they would be likely to work past 62, down from 55.4% in March 2020, on the eve of the Covid-19 pandemic. Among respondents 45 or younger, the results were similar. Roughly 48.8% saw themselves working past 62, down from 56.5% in March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Many older workers retired early in the pandemic to protect themselves from Covid-19. But economists and Fed officials thought this wave of excess retirements would be temporary.\par \par \uc2 "Assuming that the pandemic has not permanently changed the timing of future retirement decisions among younger cohorts of workers, at some point over the next few years the retired share of the population should likely stabilize and converge to what it would be expected to be in the absence of pandemic-related influences," concluded a Fed paper from 2022.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  survey suggests the pandemic may have, indeed, permanently changed how younger cohorts see retirement.\par \par \uc2 This is a reversal from the aftermath of the 2007-09 recession. The labor-force participation of those between 25 and 54 fell by almost 3 percentage points between 2008 and 2015. But participation among older workers increased slightly. Today, participation among younger workers is at the highest level since 2002, while for those over 55 it has matched the lowest point since 2007.\par \par \uc2 This is a good thing in many ways. A rip-roaring stock market has fattened America's 401(k) plans. And higher wages and multiple rounds of fiscal stimulus during the pandemic have given many people more financial security. These people may find they don't need to work well into their golden years to enjoy a comfortable retirement.\par \par \uc2 But retiring at younger ages could have consequences for monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 For starters, the overall size of the labor force could be smaller than Fed officials anticipated, all else being equal. That could weigh on overall economic growth and put added pressure on pension programs and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 It also could be harder for employers to find workers, which means the labor market could be tight for years to come.\par \par \uc2 That could force businesses to keep wage gains elevated to attract more younger workers. And that, in turn, could make it somewhat more difficult to achieve and keep inflation at the Fed's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240625ek6p0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
