\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- The Economist Who Figured Out What Makes Workers Tick --- A former college dropout, \uc2 David Autor\uc2  is now helping shape the U.S. response to the biggest issues affecting American labor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 1,353 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The man whose thinking helped change our understanding of the American labor market lives in perpetual motion. If he isn't working, he's disassembling a broken kitchen mixer, or teaching himself how to splice rope, or listening to podcasts at double speed while doing the workout routine he calls his "seven-minute twerkout."\par \par \uc2 David Autor\uc2  cut a peripatetic path through most of his 20s as a one-time college dropout and self-taught mechanic, before he stumbled into economics.\par \par \uc2 "I fell into it assbackwards," he said.\par \par \uc2 Today, his work is helping shape how the White House is approaching the biggest labor issues from responding to the threat of a "China Shock 2.0" to thinking about the economic impacts of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Autor has shown how the rise of the computer was hurting middle-class jobs. He sounded the alarm that workers in the South were getting pulverized by Chinese imports, years before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected president, playing off this fear.\par \par \uc2 Now, Autor's research has taken an unexpectedly optimistic turn: He has shown how, after the pandemic struck, low-wage workers have started catching up. He holds a hopeful view of AI, arguing that it could help low-skilled workers.\par \par \uc2 "To me, the labor market is the central institution of any society," said Autor, 60 years old. "The fastest way to improve people's welfare is to improve the labor market."\par \par \uc2 Lawrence Katz, a \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  economist who has trained a generation of labor economists, said Autor has "probably been the most insightful and influential scholar of the labor market" in decades.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration's efforts to shield domestic manufacturers from a rising tide of Chinese imports has been guided by Autor's work. The Council of Economic Advisers' annual report to the president this year is riddled with references to his research.\par \par \uc2 Autor grew up in Newton, Mass., the middle child of two psychologists, Sanford and Sherry Autor. He remembers himself as a painfully shy introvert. His mother recalls a kid who loved his dog and spent hours taking things apart.\par \par \uc2 He dropped out of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  after three semesters, did computer consulting work, bought a motorcycle and taught himself how to fix it. After a couple of years, he returned to college and studied psychology and computer science at \uc2 Tufts University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When Autor graduated in 1989, just shy of 25, he wasn't sure what to do. So he drove across the country in an eight-forward-speed, stick-shift 1980 Dodge Colt RS that cost $250. On the way, he heard a radio segment about a program that taught computer skills at Glide Memorial Church in San Francisco's Tenderloin district.\par \par \uc2 Autor started working there, and his views on labor began taking form. The people he was working with were poor, and what they desired was basic: decent jobs, stable lives.\par \par \uc2 After three years he started graduate studies in public policy at \uc2 Harvard's Kennedy School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There, at age 29, Autor took his first economics class. It blew his mind.\par \par \uc2 "This is the thing that puts together the questions I care about with methods that I like," Autor recalled. "Why didn't anyone tell me about this?"\par \par \uc2 As he prepped for the job market, a fellow Ph.D. student asked him whether he was really going to wear his gecko earring to an interview. Of course, Autor replied. He'd gotten it when he and his wife, Marika Tatsutani, were first dating and split a pair.\par \par \uc2 He was surprised to get an offer from MIT's economics department. It often hires Harvard's economics Ph.D.s -- but not public policy Ph.D.s.\par \par \uc2 During Autor's early days as an economist, an "aha!" moment came during a series of conversations with MIT's Frank Levy and Harvard's Richard Murnane. At one point, Levy suggested they could measure how hard a task is for humans by measuring how much code it would take for a computer to accomplish it. The premise: what is hard for computers is hard for humans.\par \par \uc2 Autor pointed out that it was the opposite. Adding up hundreds of numbers is hard for people but trivial for a computer. Figuring whether something left on a desk is trash is easy for people but enormously hard for computers.\par \par \uc2 They concluded that computers are good at tasks that are cognitive but also routine -- the type of work done by bookkeepers and switchboard operators.\par \par \uc2 As a result, computers were displacing jobs that were once tickets to the middle class for workers without college degrees. Meanwhile, more educated workers were benefiting from the increased productivity computers were giving them. Low-paid service work, like cleaning the homes of those educated workers, wasn't hurt either: Computers couldn't put sheets on beds.\par \par \uc2 Their paper was published in 2003. It has been cited in 129 academic publications so far this year alone.\par \par \uc2 He put in long hours. He and Tatsutani had young children at home. "My kids think I'm a good parent," he said. "But my wife and I both know that I wasn't when they were young."\par \par \uc2 A few years after Autor got tenure, the 2008 financial crisis hit. Construction work dried up. Unemployment soared. And then there was China's rise as a global manufacturing juggernaut. Economic orthodoxy held that the benefits of trade far outweighed any drawbacks.\par \par \uc2 With economists David Dorn and Gordon Hanson, Autor dug in. What they found: People in communities competing with Chinese imports, such as furniture-making towns or at textile-manufacturing centers concentrated in the South, were losing their jobs. Often, they were forced onto food stamps and disability.\par \par \uc2 The research, first released in 2011, was controversial. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , campaigning for the presidency in 2016, successfully tapped into exactly that frustration. Indeed, later work would show that the more affected an area was by "the China Shock," the larger the rightward shift among that area's white voters.\par \par \uc2 When Covid arrived and the unemployment rate shot to nearly 15%, Autor found something he didn't expect: As the job market came back, low-wage workers' pay rose at a faster clip than their higher-paid counterparts'.\par \par \uc2 Working with \uc2 Arindrajit Dube\uc2  and Annie McGrew from the \uc2 University of Massachusetts\uc2 , Amherst, Autor sought to understand why.\par \par \uc2 Labor economists have long recognized that low-wage, less-educated workers often aren't well matched into jobs where they can be more productive and make more money. The dishwasher at the restaurant on the corner of State and Main might not know there is a better-run restaurant a few blocks away that pays more.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic changed all that. First, it shook poorer workers out of their jobs. Then, when the economy reopened, many of them found better work that paid more. Government relief might have helped, too. With more money in the bank, people had the luxury to take the time to find a good job.\par \par \uc2 The outcome: The still-large pay gap between the highest-paid workers and the lowest has substantially narrowed.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to AI and workers, Autor is hopeful. People with less education will be able to do more work that can now be performed only by highly trained elite workers, such as computer coding. "It's a mistake at this point to think that it's just more of the same," Autor said. "I don't think it is, and that's great."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 David Autor\par \par \uc2 -- Sails job: Autor got hooked on sailing at 14 when a friend took him on the Charles River and they capsized.\par \par \uc2 -- Calculus: He first studied it at Harvard at age 30, with a bunch of 18-year-olds.\par \par \uc2 -- Influential: The Council of Economic Advisers' annual report to the president references 10 of Autor's publications.\par \par \uc2 -- Inked: On a father-daughter trip to the tattoo parlor, Autor got a gecko on his calf to match his earring.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240622ek6m0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
