\par\par \b \uc2 The Fed Can Take Its Time Taming Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason Furman \par \uc2 991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two years ago the six-month annual pace of core inflation was 6%. Now it has fallen to 3%, without a recession. Does that mean we can count on inflation easing painlessly to 2% and the Fed can begin cutting rates in anticipation of an imminent soft landing? While the past several years fostered humility among economists, the much-reviled traditional model for inflation forecasting still provides a valuable framework for thinking about the rise, fall and future of inflation. This framework offers many reasons why the last mile of disinflation could be the hardest, validating the Fed's caution on rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The expectations-augmented Phillips curve has been the workhorse framework for thinking about inflation since Milton Friedman and Ned Phelps developed it 50 years ago. It holds that underlying inflation depends on both inertia from past wage and price growth and expectations for the future. Actual inflation will be higher or lower than underlying inflation to the degree that labor markets are tight or loose. And like all models, it can fluctuate owing to supply shocks such as changes in global oil prices or spurts of productivity.\par \par \uc2 As confounding and imprecise as this model has been, none other has done better. The theory has a lot going for it, raising queries such as these: Would wage and price pressures be greater when the unemployment rate is 1% compared with when it is 10%? Would price pressures depend on what prices were previously expected to do?\par \par \uc2 To use this framework to understand the disinflation we have experienced and the challenges ahead, let's move in reverse order, starting with supply shocks. Bad luck, including supply-chain bottlenecks and higher commodity prices worldwide after Russia's invasion of Ukraine, stoked cost-push inflation in 2021 and 2022. That raised headline inflation and bled into core inflation, which excludes volatile food and energy prices. At the time, many correctly considered that portion of the excess inflation to be transitory.\par \par \uc2 Now we are in the reverse situation. Core inflation has been higher than headline inflation for most of the past year -- suggesting that transitory factors are temporarily lowering inflation and it would be foolish to count on them to keep doing so.\par \par \uc2 The next piece of the framework is labor-market tightness. Here two twists to the model can help us understand the rise and fall of inflation. I've been working with the twists for years; they aren't just after-the-fact examples grafted on to the model.\par \par \uc2 The first twist is not to look solely at the unemployment rate but also to consider job openings. The ratio of job openings to the unemployment rate peaked at two openings per unemployed person in 2022. That's the tightest labor market ever according to this measure, though it has since eased back to a more normal 1.2. This labor-market loosening has been even larger than after the global financial crisis. Fortunately, this time it happened mostly through businesses paring job openings rather than a surge in unemployment.\par \par \uc2 The second twist is that the inflationary process seems to be nonlinear. Going from extremely tight to somewhat tight makes a big difference, as employers chasing a tiny number of workers need to bid up wages, and corresponding upward pressure pushes up prices. But different gradations of tightness in a more normal range make much less of a difference. A simulation I released in 2022 forecast that it would take a 4% unemployment rate to lower inflation to 3% but would require unemployment rising all the way to 6.7% to get inflation down to 2%. I'm considerably more optimistic today, but the general principle still holds -- the first part of disinflation requires the least pain but it gets harder as you go.\par \par \uc2 The final part of the framework is the combination of inertia and expectations. The Fed earned a lot of credibility for Chairman Paul Volcker's effort in getting inflation down in the 1980s and his successors' efforts to keep it low. The Fed's 2022 pivot to aggressive rate increases, well beyond what even I could have imagined possible, helped ensure that it kept this credibility. While Fed critics abound in the media, this is cheap talk compared with the markets, where investors bet real money and largely have expected medium-term inflation to be at the Fed's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 Inertia also plays a role. Wage growth and price growth feed into each other, and both of those are running 1 to 1.5 percentage points higher than before Covid. Shorter-term measures of inflation expectations are also elevated. While this may not be a full-blown wage-price spiral, wage-price persistence will keep inflation higher until something pushes it in a different direction.\par \par \uc2 There is reason to expect inflation to continue falling. The labor-market loosening will work with a lag, and wages and prices appear to be in a diminishing dynamic. I believe underlying inflation is in the 2.5% to 3% range. Getting it much below that could be hard and may not even be needed. But there is a meaningful risk, maybe 1 in 3, that if demand stays strong inflation could stay above 3%, which would be a big blow to the credibility that has helped make \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 's task so much easier than Volcker's.\par \par \uc2 Given strong U.S. job growth, the Fed can afford to spend several more months assessing just how big the reflation risk is.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Furman, a professor of the practice of economic policy at Harvard, was \uc2 chairman of the White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 , 2013-17.\par \p