\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Gets Court Backing in Labor Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erin Mulvaney, Heather Haddon and Jan Wolfe \par \uc2 606 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Thursday backed \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in a battle with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , potentially making it more difficult for the agency to take action on behalf of workers fired during union drives.\par \par \uc2 The court, in an opinion by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , said lower courts followed an approach that was too favorable to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  when they ordered the reinstatement of seven terminated \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers in Memphis, Tenn., who had been involved with a unionizing campaign. The employees alleged they were unlawfully fired after they started the union effort. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said it fired them for violating company policies.\par \par \uc2 In a separate decision handed down on Thursday, the court ruled against a California man who wanted to trademark a derogatory slogan about former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for use on T-shirts and hats.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  case is part of a yearslong clash between the coffee giant and workers who have sought to unionize hundreds of stores. The Memphis workers began organizing in 2022. As part of the campaign, the workers invited a news crew to visit the store to promote the effort. They were fired soon after bringing in a television crew after hours.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , which has fielded hundreds of unfair labor practice charges against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , filed a complaint over the dismissed Memphis workers and asked a federal judge to reinstate them while the board considered the case. The judge agreed and an appeals court affirmed that decision.\par \par \uc2 Thomas said the legal standard the lower courts used gave too much deference to the agency, adding that it was "hard to imagine" how the board could ever lose under that test. The court sent the case back for additional proceedings. Seven other justices agreed with the ruling in full.\par \par \uc2 Judges had been using different standards for deciding when to issue injunctions that required the reinstatement of workers. Thursday's decision means the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  in some jurisdictions will need to clear higher legal hurdles to win.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesman said consistent federal standards help employees know their rights and create uniform labor practices across the country. The company is making progress in negotiations with unionized employees and aims to ratify labor agreements with represented stores this year, he said.\par \par \uc2 Of the seven employees, five are still working at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , the company said.\par \par \uc2 Workers United, the main union organizing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's U.S. stores, called the ruling "egregious," and said the company should have dropped the case once it agreed to move forward with bargaining. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  declined to comment, but referred to an April statement in which general counsel \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2  said the board had been successful in the past under various legal tests.\par \par \uc2 In Thursday's trademark case, the high court said the \uc2 U.S. Patent and Trademark Office\uc2  was right to reject a California man's effort to claim rights to the phrase "Trump too small." The case, Vidal v. Elster, dates back to early 2016, when Sen. \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  (R., Fla.), then running for president, said then-candidate Trump had small hands.\par \par \uc2 California-based lawyer Steve Elster sought to trademark "Trump too small," which he said was meant to convey that "some features of President Trump and his policies are diminutive."\par \par \uc2 The patent office rejected his application, citing a provision of the federal Lanham Act that prohibits trademarking a living person's name without their authorization. The court's lead opinion, written by Thomas, said the prohibition on trademarking someone else's name doesn't discriminate against particular viewpoints and is consistent with the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240614ek6e0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
