\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 A Reckoning for Biden's Labor Chief\b0\par\par\uc2 756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden is using executive power to change labor law to favor unions, and his \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) has been the tip of his spear. That's why he wants the Senate to rush through the renomination of a regulator who might as well work for the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House plans to renominate \uc2 Lauren McFerran\uc2  to chair the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , hoping to keep her in the seat for another five years. Her current term expires in December, but Mr. Biden wants the Senate to move fast. She'll need the support of at least 50 Democrats, and this might be a tough vote for some incumbents running for re-election this year.\par \par \uc2 That's because the agency has shed any veneer of fairness during her tenure, trampling the rights of small and large businesses. A series of rulings since 2021 have stretched the protections in labor law wide enough to make workers virtually unfireable, as long as they belong to a union or wish to join one.\par \par \uc2 One target of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's abuse is Stern Produce, a grocery distributor in Arizona. In 2021 the company issued a warning to two of its drivers for serious workplace violations: covering up a surveillance camera, and a threatening joke about a colleague's sexual orientation. Both employees admitted to their actions, but Ms. McFerran ruled that the company's warnings were illegal intimidation because the two workers had tried to unionize years earlier.\par \par \uc2 Dozens of rulings fit the same pattern, with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  tearing up the rights of businesses or non-union workers. Its 2023 Cemex ruling is among the most destructive, making it easier for unions to gain recognition without winning workers' support on a secret ballot. Ms. McFerran called the new standard "free and fair, and efficient," but not so much for employers, nor the workers who will be pressured to join unions against their will.\par \par \uc2 These decisions send a clear message to employers: Roll over for union activists, or the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  will turn any management decision into a labor violation. That strategy was made explicit by \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2 , whom President Biden appointed as general counsel in 2021 and who took office with a list of employer protections she vowed to overturn. Ms. McFerran has rubber stamped this agenda in her rulings, along with the two other Democratic members in the board's 3-1 majority.\par \par \uc2 Appellate courts have withering words for the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's recent turn. Overturning the Stern Produce decision in March, Judge Raymond Randolph called the board's reasoning "nonsense" and scored the majority for twisting the law.\par \par \uc2 "Years ago the Board took an expansive view of the scope of the Act," he wrote, referring to the National Labor Relations Act that anchors federal labor law. "Over time, it 'presse[d] the rationale of that expansion to the limits of its logic.'" In other words, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has unbound itself to aid the interests of unions.\par \par \uc2 In that case as well as recent complaints against \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  and others, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  board reversed a previous ruling by one of its own administrative law judges who had ruled for the company. Progressives used to blast GOP-majority boards for overruling the agency's supposedly neutral jurists. Now the board's Democrats have gone so far that even career employees want to rein them in.\par \par \uc2 Congress has noted the shift, and even some Democrats believe the agency has swung too far. Sens. Joe Manchin, \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  and Angus King joined Republicans in voting to rescind the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's recent joint-employer rule, which breaks precedent to help unions organize franchise businesses. President Biden vetoed the bipartisan Congressional Review Act resolution and let the harmful rule take effect.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has timed his appointments to the labor board to minimize resistance. He broke with tradition by not choosing a Republican to fill an open seat when the previous chairman, picked by President Trump, retired in 2022. Instead Mr. Biden waited until now to select a Republican at the same time he has renominated Ms. McFerran. He hopes presenting the two as a package will make it easier for vulnerable Democrats to approve Ms. McFerran.\par \par \uc2 It's an offer the Senate should refuse. Reapproving the sitting chairman would be business as usual in a Senate that has whooped through too many of Mr. Biden's progressive nominees. The economy and the rights of workers will suffer if Ms. McFerran is confirmed again after her demonstrably lawless record.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240611ek6b00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
