\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Trump Wants to Make Workers' Tips Tax-Free\b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Rubin and Vivian Salama \par \uc2 514 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's proposal to make workers' tips nontaxable introduces an untested idea to raise take-home pay that would encourage businesses and employees to recharacterize taxed wages as untaxed tips.\par \par \uc2 Such a move could further complicate Americans' messy relationships with tips, which have spread to new corners of the economy in recent years even as consumers express growing exasperation with requests for voluntary payments on top of prices.\par \par \uc2 Trump, the presumptive Republican presidential nominee, announced the proposal Sunday at a rally in Nevada, trying to appeal to that swing state's service-industry workers. Trump narrowly lost Nevada in 2020 and 2016, and the state is seen as closely competitive again this year.\par \par \uc2 "For those hotel workers and people that get tips, you're going to be very happy, because when I get to office, we are going to not charge taxes on tips," he said. "We're going to do that right away."\par \par \uc2 Such an idea, which would require approval from Congress, would change the way many workers are taxed and could be challenging for lawmakers to write and for tax authorities to enforce. The idea isn't fleshed out and would likely reduce federal revenue by billions of dollars annually.\par \par \uc2 Employers reported that workers received more than $38 billion in tips in tax year 2018, according to \uc2 IRS\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign hasn't provided additional details. The idea wasn't something that the Trump administration considered or studied during his first term as president, said Dave Kautter, who was the Treasury Department's top tax-policy official.\par \par \uc2 The proposal would create a two-tiered labor market where tipped workers would gain a significant advantage over other low-wage employees because they could potentially avoid Social Security taxes, Medicare taxes and federal income taxes.\par \par \uc2 Employers, who owe payroll taxes on both tips and wages, would have an incentive to keep wages low and encourage tips instead. Trump himself owns service-industry businesses.\par \par \uc2 "We have to think about the unforeseen consequences of this," said \uc2 Ernie Tedeschi\uc2 , a former Biden administration economist who is now at Yale University's Budget Lab. "Money finds a way to game the system."\par \par \uc2 Depending on how it is written and implemented, the tax advantage could push tipping culture into new spaces. An auto-body shop could restrain its prices and wages and strongly encourage tipping as a way to get untaxed income to workers.\par \par \uc2 Tips are now taxable income -- a box on the W-2 says "wages, tips and other compensation" -- but the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  has long struggled to get employers and workers to report that income accurately.\par \par \uc2 Trump's idea would make tipping a more valuable form of compensation -- and thus increase tipping, said Kyle Pomerleau, a tax expert at the conservative-leaning \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This is similar to the exemption for employer-sponsored health insurance, which is an exempt form of income -- except in this case there is no policy rationale," he said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich467\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7005
