\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Trucking Payrolls Slide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 219 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Trucking companies are shedding jobs as a downturn in freight demand persists.\par \par \uc2 The trucking industry lost a seasonally adjusted 5,400 jobs in May from a month earlier, according to preliminary figures released by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  on Friday. The pullback was the largest single monthly decline in the sector since August 2023 and left trucking payrolls down 29,600 jobs over the past year. The decline came even as the broader U.S. economy added 272,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 Truckers are working through one of the most prolonged downturns in the U.S. domestic freight market in recent memory, as an oversupply of drivers, lured into the industry during the Covid-driven boom in freight demand, competes for cargo and pushes down prices.\par \par \uc2 Some industry observers said the declining employment numbers are a sign that small companies and independent owner-operators that started operations during the high-demand period are now leaving the market as profits recede.\par \par \uc2 The freight sector has struggled for about two years amid weak freight demand and low cargo rates.\par \par \uc2 The slump contributed to the collapse of several large companies, including Yellow, one of the nation's oldest carriers, and \uc2 Convoy\uc2 , a technology-focused freight broker that matched truckers with available loads.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240608ek680000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
