\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- If These Resumes Could Talk: One Way Not to Get the Job: Interview From Your Bed --- Job candidates don't always dress to impress, but most know not to show up for the interview in their pajamas -- or to hide a helper in the covers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francesca Fontana \par \uc2 757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [A Wall Street Journal feature in which recruiters and headhunters share their wildest and most interesting stories.]\par \par \uc2 Q: What is an instance where a Zoom job interview went horribly wrong?\par \par \uc2 Make yourself comfortable\par \par \uc2 I like to coach my candidates, because a lot of them haven't interviewed in a long time and even if they have it's not something they do a lot of, so it's not like people are experts at interviewing.\par \par \uc2 This one candidate was up for a legal-secretary position. I was doing a mock interview with her on Zoom, and she was in her bedroom. I always tell candidates to not interview in their bedroom: It's too personal, people get uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 But she said she didn't have another place to interview. So, though I was reluctant to, I said, "Alright, just make sure it's really tidy, that there's nothing out of place, nothing personal showing. Make sure you look as professional as you can."\par \par \uc2 The candidate does her interview, and afterward I get the call from the HR manager. She had tidied up the bedroom, like I told her to. But she interviewed in her pajamas -- and sitting in the bed!\par \par \uc2 I was horribly embarrassed. I didn't even call the candidate back to ask her about it. I mean, what was I going to say? "What were you thinking?" Obviously she wasn't!\par \par \uc2 -- Chere Estrin, Estrin Legal Staffing\par \par \uc2 Undercover assistance\par \par \uc2 I've been recruiting for this client for over a decade, and an HR executive told me about a panel interview for this senior-level position. Everyone on the client side, they were all in a conference room to interview this candidate over video.\par \par \uc2 When the candidate's video popped up, the team noticed that they were interviewing from a bedroom, and there was an unmade bed in the background. The bedding was all jumbled up, and it looked like there was a body hidden under the blankets -- if you looked at it long enough, you just swore it moved.\par \par \uc2 As they went through this interview, they found that as they were posing questions to the candidate, the candidate was getting interview answers from the pile of bedding!\par \par \uc2 It wasn't that they could hear someone giving the candidate answers loud and clear. There was just too much of a delay after they asked a question. Ordinarily you pose a question to the candidate and the candidate will pop back with an answer almost instantaneously, but there was just too much of a delay. And when people talk, they move a little. The bedding was moving -- and it shouldn't have been moving.\par \par \uc2 They ended up making the interview a lot shorter than it was originally planned to be.\par \par \uc2 It was the most incredible thing they had ever seen.\par \par \uc2 -- Jane Snipes, Northstar Recruiting\par \par \uc2 Background check\par \par \uc2 Candidates forget to check what's in the background of their Zoom calls -- like this one candidate who had a giant poster of an Iron Maiden album cover on the wall, with this big skeleton carrying the British flag -- so we tell everybody to make sure it's a neutral background.\par \par \uc2 Another one had a bookshelf behind them, and on it was a copy of "The Rise and Fall of the Third Reich." And the spine has a swastika on it. I mean, there's nothing wrong with owning the book, of course. It's a history book! But not something you want in the background.\par \par \uc2 -- Shahan Avedian, \uc2 Yoh Services\par \par \uc2 Weird weather\par \par \uc2 We like to ask candidates questions about whatever place they're based in -- it's a nice way to corroborate someone's location, if they actually can name landmarks or cities or talk about the weather.\par \par \uc2 Recently, we were talking to a candidate who said she was based in Michigan. We're also based in Michigan, in Ann Arbor. We asked where she was, and she said, "Bloomingfield Hills." But the city's name is actually "Bloomfield Hills," so that was a bit of a red flag.\par \par \uc2 So I asked her, "Oh, is it snowing near you?" And she was like, "Yeah!"\par \par \uc2 It was not snowing.\par \par \uc2 -- Benjamin Taub, Dataspace\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 These interviews have been edited and condensed for length and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240608ek680003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
