\par\par \b \uc2 Jobs Market Makes Show Of Resilience\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Goldfarb \par \uc2 980 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. job growth burst past expectations last month even as the unemployment rate edged up to 4%, presenting a mixed view of a labor market that has generally been cooling while staying hotter than many anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 272,000 new jobs in May, the Labor Department reported on Friday, more than in April and well above the 190,000 that economists had expected. The figure doesn't include agriculture jobs and is adjusted for seasonal swings like extra hiring around holidays. Average hourly earnings also topped forecasts, rising 4.1% from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Overall, analysts said that the hiring boost highlighted the continued resilience of the U.S. economy, which has defied expectations as businesses keep hiring, wages keep rising and consumers keep spending in the face of steeply higher borrowing costs and persistent inflation.\par \par \uc2 The one caveat in Friday's otherwise-strong report was the unemployment rate, which ticked up from 3.9% in April. It was the first time in more than two years that the jobless rate hit 4%. It also marked the extension of a steady climb higher; the rate was as low as 3.4% last year.\par \par \uc2 Yet unemployment remains low by historical standards and has shown little sign of making a big jump. The unemployment rate has been at or below 4% for 30 months, something most working-age Americans had never before experienced.\par \par \uc2 Looking across a broad array of economic data, "you do see a general path toward softening in the labor market," said Jonathan Pingle, U.S. economist at \uc2 Natixis\uc2 . However, he said, it is hard not to conclude after Friday's report that "we have a considerably stronger labor market than we expected a month ago."\par \par \uc2 The fact that the unemployment rate ticked higher while the economy added so many jobs might seem impossible. But the monthly payroll figures and the unemployment rate are derived from two separate surveys and sometimes send conflicting messages.\par \par \uc2 Some economists have said that surprisingly large payroll gains could be partly attributable to a surge in immigration that has added to the number of job seekers. That could be causing the workforce to grow without leading to increased competition for employees.\par \par \uc2 Still, the unemployment rate is based on a smaller survey and can jump around from month to month. As a result, investors were at least initially focused Friday on the jobs and wage numbers, which caused many to scale back bets that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates this year.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting that view, bond yields surged after the report, with the yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note jumping above 4.4% from under 4.3% Thursday. Stock moves were muted, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average falling 87 points and the S&P 500 and Nasdaq Composite also inching lower. All three indexes logged weekly gains.\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate futures showed Friday that investors now see a greater than 40% chance that the Fed will hold rates steady past its September meeting, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2  data. That was up from 31% Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Several recent reports have hinted at some weakness in the economy. Data on retail sales, overall consumer spending, construction and industrial production have all come in below economists' expectations.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Friday's report showed a broad rebound in payroll growth. Government jobs increased 43,000 last month, returning to the steady pace of gains that had paused in April. Private-sector hiring also climbed, with a noticeable pickup among leisure and hospitality businesses.\par \par \uc2 Most economists have remained optimistic about the U.S. economy's near-term outlook, believing it is normalizing after a surprisingly hot stretch last year, rather than deteriorating in a more concerning fashion.\par \par \uc2 Yet Americans have remained gloomy about the economy, despite wage increases and low unemployment. Many have felt the sting of inflation and feel bitter about price increases for everything from groceries to airline tickets.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials, meanwhile, have publicly kept much of their focus less on the jobs number and more on inflation, which has leveled off this year after a sharp decline last year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely expected to keep rates unchanged at its meeting next week. Officials will submit their first formal interest-rate forecasts since March, when a narrow majority reaffirmed projections to cut rates three times this year.\par \par \uc2 The latest jobs report likely wouldn't be decisive in changing the views of Fed officials, some analysts said, in part because of the divergence between the unemployment rate and the payroll numbers.\par \par \uc2 "It feels a little to me like there's something for the doves and the hawks here," said \uc2 Michael Pugliese\uc2 , a senior economist at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . The central bank will likely remain in "a wait-and-see mode," with officials paying close attention to inflation data, he added.\par \par \uc2 There are some unique features of the current jobs market, one being an unusual lack of turnover, with employers hiring fewer people but also laying off fewer workers than is typical, according to separate Labor Department data released earlier this past week.\par \par \uc2 After having a hard time finding workers when the economy was recovering from the pandemic, businesses now have appeared reluctant to let them go. That has persisted despite higher interest rates, which might normally be expected to lead to a downward spiral of job losses and household belt tightening.\par \par \uc2 For the Fed, a key measure of labor-market strength is worker pay, because faster wage growth is generally associated with higher inflation. Wages across the economy, though, are difficult to measure, and Fed officials have recently been highlighting actual inflation data more than other factors that could influence those numbers.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department will release its next report on the consumer-price index on Wednesday, hours before the Fed concludes its policy meeting.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich379\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal5685
