\par\par \b \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  Seeks Support For Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 917 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  has launched a charm offensive to win support for its planned acquisition of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  in a bid to counter the deal's staunchest critics.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  shareholders overwhelmingly approved the company's $14.1 billion takeover in April, but the deal remains bogged down by federal regulatory review and a raft of opposition. Leaders of the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  union, some members of Congress and \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2 , \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's main rival, have panned the purchase. President Biden has expressed skepticism about it.\par \par \uc2 Takahiro Mori\uc2 , vice chairman of the Tokyo-based steelmaker, met this past week with business executives and government leaders in the Pittsburgh area, which includes \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's headquarters and its Mon Valley Works operation. Mori hosted a dinner and a presentation for about 150 people that lasted more than two hours.\par \par \uc2 "This transaction is about growth," Mori said in an interview. "We're going to share all the fruits of our technology."\par \par \uc2 The company said it would invest at least $1.4 billion to improve the performance of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's older mills, which also include operations in Indiana. \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  spends more than $500 million a year on research and development, compared with about $40 million by \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , according to the companies. Mori said \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's chief technology officer will visit the Mon Valley Works as part of the planning for production improvements.\par \par \uc2 Residents of some of the small towns surrounding the Mon Valley plants have complained for years about air pollution and a lack of investment in maintenance. Mori has pledged to use hydrogen energy to reduce Mon Valley's carbon-dioxide emissions from burning coal in blast furnaces.\par \par \uc2 Elaina Skiba, borough manager of Glassport, Pa., said she and other community officials have been skeptical about the acquisition and concerned about the future for the Mon Valley operation, but she left the presentation with more confidence in \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's plans.\par \par \uc2 "Nippon has no intention of closing this mill," she said. "They want it to succeed. They're not going to put this kind of money on the table to close it."\par \par \uc2 Mori is planning several stateside visits this summer, but didn't meet with union leaders in the latest visit. The union is seeking commitments from \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  on specific plant upgrades and has been dissatisfied with the Japanese company's attempts to maintain the current labor agreement with \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The union said Mori's visit was a "desperate last gasp" by the two companies to save the deal and accused \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  of trying to escape its obligations under the union's contract with \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The union endorsed \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2 ' attempt to buy \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  last year. In turn, \uc2 Cleveland-Cliffs\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Lourenco Goncalves\uc2  is backing the union's opposition to the sale to \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 . He said recently that the deal is dead without the union's support.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  is seeking to close its purchase by year's end, pushing back the deadline by a few months. It has said it would refrain from plant closings and layoffs through the end of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's union labor agreement in 2026.\par \par \uc2 A weakening American steel market threatens to add uncertainty to \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's investment plans. Falling steel demand and low prices would ratchet up the pressure on the company to conserve cash and reduce expenses.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing activity has receded in some steel-consuming industries, including parts of the construction industry, commercial trucks, farm machinery and home appliances. Contributing to the downturn is the absence in recent years of panic buying caused by Covid-related supply bottlenecks, Russia's war in Ukraine and other market shocks that drove up prices.\par \par \uc2 Some steel mill executives describe reluctant buyers as being on a diet. The U.S. spot-market price for hot-rolled coiled sheet steel was $750 a ton on Friday, down nearly 30% from a year earlier, according to S&P Global Commodity Insights. The benchmark price sank to $690 in September when a strike against U.S. automakers chilled steel buying but went as high as nearly $2,000 a ton in 2021.\par \par \uc2 A decline in shipments hurt \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's first-quarter performance, with sales falling 7% from a year earlier and net income shrinking 14%.\par \par \uc2 Mori said a soft American steel market wouldn't sidetrack spending to improve \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 . \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  executives have been dealing with a yearslong slump in Japan, where the company is the country's largest steel producer.\par \par \uc2 Japan's estimated steel consumption -- which includes domestic steel production and imports minus exports -- sank 17% from 2019 to 2023, according to the \uc2 Japan Iron and Steel Federation\uc2 . \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  has closed some plants in Japan in response to falling demand.\par \par \uc2 American steel consumption slipped 7% during the same period, according to the \uc2 American Iron and Steel Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  has the economics worked out on the deal, but the politics is a work in progress.\par \par \uc2 Biden, who won Pennsylvania in the 2020 election, has signaled opposition to foreign ownership of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , though he hasn't explicitly said he would block the deal, which is now under national-security review by a Treasury Department-led committee.\par \par \uc2 The presumptive Republican presidential nominee, Donald Trump, has publicly criticized the deal, which could become an important issue in Pennsylvania, seen as a battleground state in the presidential contest.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich443\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6645
