\par\par \b \uc2 World News: Strike Threat by Border Agents In Canada Imperils U.s. Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira \par \uc2 437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA -- The union representing Canadian border agents said over 90% of its members voted to go on strike as soon as next month, posing a risk to billions of dollars in daily cross-border commercial trade between the U.S. and Canada.\par \par \uc2 A strike could bring truck traffic to a standstill at designated crossings along the 4,000-mile U.S.-Canadian border, and marks a second threat this summer to the North American supply-chain network. Canada could face a possible simultaneous strike at the country's two main railroads, \uc2 Canadian National Railway\uc2  and Canadian Pacific Kansas City, sometime in July.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\uc2  warned this week such an event could have "significant impacts" for agriculture trade, producers and consumers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Public Service Alliance of Canada\uc2  said its 9,000 members who work at the \uc2 Canada Border Services Agency\uc2  would be in a legal strike position once a federal labor-board committee delivers a report recommending how the employer, the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  and the union can settle differences on a new agreement.\par \par \uc2 Canada said 90% of the border agency's employees are deemed essential workers, so they need to stay on the job during so-called strike action. However, as they did in a nearly daylong partial strike in August 2021, border agents would conduct a work-to-rule campaign, which triggered a decline in productivity and long delays to process shipments and travelers at Canadian land crossings, airports and shipping ports.\par \par \uc2 During the 2021 labor disruption, commercial truckers in the U.S. faced delays of up to four hours to enter Canada at major land crossings in Michigan, Vermont, New York, North Dakota, Montana and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  data estimates that $2.6 billion of goods and services crosses the U.S.-Canada border daily.\par \par \uc2 The union said it is seeking an increase in wages that are aligned with other law-enforcement agencies across the country, and improvements in retirement benefits and hours of work.\par \par \uc2 Canada's Treasury Board, which handles day-to-day administrative operations and labor negotiations, said that although it recognizes that workers have the right to strike, "at this time it is unnecessary. We are ready and willing to return to the bargaining table at any time."\par \par \uc2 The decision by border agents to take possible strike action in June "adds another element of significant risk for the flow of goods between Canada and the U.S.," said Pascal Chan, senior director of transportation, infrastructure and construction at the Canadian Chamber of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240525ek5p0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
