\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Finding a Job Takes Longer for College Grads --- Companies are rethinking their needs for entry-level talent\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Pisani and Lindsay Ellis \par \uc2 1,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Class of 2024 is about to join a job market nearly as turbulent as their college years.\par \par \uc2 Employers plan to hire 5.8% fewer new graduates than they did last year, according to a spring survey of 226 employers by the \uc2 National Association of Colleges and Employers\uc2 . And what those bosses want from entry-level workers is changing, students and recruiters say, from years of experience to sophisticated artificial-intelligence skills. Some companies say AI is taking over part of the work fresh graduates used to do.\par \par \uc2 It's the latest challenge for these students, who began their studies with Covid-19 lockdowns and finished them amid protests that upended campus life nationwide.\par \par \uc2 "I didn't think it would be so hard to even get a screening interview," said Daniel Cooper, a computer-engineering major who graduated from the \uc2 Georgia Institute of Technology\uc2 . The 22-year-old accepted a software-engineering job earlier this spring, but the offer was rescinded after the company announced layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the job market remains strong, and recruiting executives say new graduate-hires benefit from steady wage growth over the past few years. But it's taking graduating seniors longer to find white-collar roles. By April 2023, more than a third had accepted a full-time job and stopped looking further, according to research and analytics firm Veris Insights. This year, just under a quarter had.\par \par \uc2 Cooper has applied to about three dozen other software engineering jobs since his offer evaporated, resulting in two interviews but no new offers. Though he's had eight internships, some of the job applications he's filled out ask for years of work experience, excluding internships, he said. That puts him at zero.\par \par \uc2 HealthEdge\uc2 , an insurance-software company, is among the companies hiring fewer new graduates this year. It's bringing on five new graduates in the U.S. compared with 20 in 2023. Overall, it's hiring fewer entry-level employees, and a greater share of them are in India, said Stefani Coleman, who leads early-talent recruiting and programming.\par \par \uc2 Demand for such jobs, meanwhile, has been overwhelming, she added. At one point, she received about 2,000 applications within 24 hours for an associate software-engineering role.\par \par \uc2 "The market has changed so drastically," she said.\par \par \uc2 The supply of computer-science majors continues to grow as hiring demand for software programming roles appears to be cooling. Two other major campus job recruiting sectors -- consulting and finance -- are also retrenching after pandemic hiring sprees.\par \par \uc2 Graduates looking for jobs must meet a higher bar because the market is already crowded with junior workers who recently lost jobs, says Jay Killough, who leads \uc2 Texas Tech University\uc2 's career center.\par \par \uc2 "Those people have experience," said Killough, "and most employers we talk to love experienced professionals."\par \par \uc2 Payton Poulston, who graduated from \uc2 Indiana University Bloomington\uc2  this month, has been looking for a job in medical-device sales since the start of her senior year. She's contacted people she met during her three internships. Her mother, who works at a hospital, has also been asking co-workers and others for job leads.\par \par \uc2 Poulston has landed interviews but no offers. The companies typically tell her they want three to five years of experience, even for jobs listed as entry-level positions.\par \par \uc2 "It might be easier to hire someone who can just come in and get the job done and know exactly how to do everything," she said.\par \par \uc2 Companies are also expecting grads to come in knowing how to use AI in their work, said Mohammad Soltanieh-ha, a clinical assistant professor at Boston University's Questrom School of Business. In class, for example, his programming students learn how to use AI to find what's wrong with their codes as opposed to spending half a day figuring it out on their own, he said.\par \par \uc2 "It's not that the AI is taking their jobs," said Soltanieh-ha. "It's somebody that knows how AI is working -- that's who is going to take their job."\par \par \uc2 AI is starting to supplant some types of traditional entry-level work. Axiologic Solutions, a government technology contractor near Washington, is deploying AI to take on tasks that new graduates previously did in its human-resources department. An AI tool can send emails and training videos to new hires. A chatbot can answer many of the questions a new hire might have.\par \par \uc2 When two people recently left the HR department, they didn't need to be replaced, said Michael Chavira, the company's managing partner and co-founder. Anyone he does hire needs to have experience using AI, since he's thinking about using it in other business areas, too, such as accounting.\par \par \uc2 "AI right now is making those entry-level positions -- I don't want to say obsolete -- but they're changing them," he said.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Neef, director of the \uc2 University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign\uc2 's career center, said she is encouraging computer science majors to look for tech roles in other industries. Smaller employers, as well as government agencies, will hire through the middle of summer, she said.\par \par \uc2 "I always ask students -- 'Who knows that you're looking for a job?' The response should be everyone," Neef said.\par \par \uc2 She has been advising students to "use a scalpel and not a machete" in contacting employers and recruiters. That means making meaningful connections, instead of spamming hiring managers with one-size-fits-all messages, and following up regularly after a first meeting. Employers want to make sure their new hires have the willingness to learn and can fit with their teams' work styles, she said.\par \par \uc2 Graduates are being advised to refrain from opining about campus protests if asked in interviews.\par \par \uc2 "Our advice is to be very neutral, like, 'Yeah, that's happening there,'" said Beth Hendler-Grunt, president of career-coaching company Next Great Step. So far, she says none of her clients have mentioned such questions coming up.\par \par \uc2 Kali Muniz, who graduated from \uc2 Texas A&M University\uc2  this month, landed a job in October, after a couple of snags.\par \par \uc2 The flight simulator company where she had previously interned told her they weren't hiring new grads because of budget cuts. She thought she had clinched an interview for a consulting job, then was surprised when she was rejected.\par \par \uc2 The job she did get -- as a financial manager for the federal government -- Muniz found by applying online. She included a recommendation from someone at her internship in her application.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know if that helped me," said the 22-year-old management major, "but I hope it did."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Chip Cutter contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240523ek5n00007\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin